window.GUIDE={"met":{"name":"Meteorology","traps":[{"n":1,"title":"Atmosphere","href":"chapters/met-01.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Atmosphere is heated by\" → <strong>heat from the earth's surface</strong> (not directly by the sun).</li>\n<li>\"Above 8 km, lower temperatures are over\" → the <strong>equator</strong>.</li>\n<li>Mother-of-pearl = <strong>nacreous</strong> = <strong>stratosphere</strong>; <strong>noctilucent</strong> = <strong>mesosphere</strong>.</li>\n<li>Negative lapse rate = temperature <strong>rising</strong> with height (inversion).</li>\n<li>Maximum heat transfer = <strong>latent heat</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":2,"title":"Atmospheric Pressure","href":"chapters/met-02.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Altimeter <strong>always measures height above the datum set on its sub-scale</strong>.</li>\n<li>Altimeter on ground reads <strong>aerodrome elevation</strong> → <strong>QNH</strong>; reads <strong>zero</strong> → <strong>QFE</strong>.</li>\n<li>Isallobars = equal <strong>pressure tendency</strong> (not equal pressure).</li>\n<li><strong>Col</strong> = two lows and two highs.</li>\n<li>Semi-diurnal variation biggest in the <strong>tropics</strong>.</li>\n<li>Steep gradient = <strong>isobars close together, strong wind</strong>.</li>\n<li>Inversion in a high = <strong>subsidence inversion</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":3,"title":"Temperature","href":"chapters/met-03.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Minimum thermometer liquid = <strong>alcohol</strong>.</li>\n<li>Surface temperature height = <strong>1.25 m</strong>.</li>\n<li>Heat = <strong>sum total</strong>; temperature = <strong>average</strong> KE.</li>\n<li>Black-body energy ∝ <strong>T⁴</strong>; peak wavelength ∝ <strong>1/T</strong>.</li>\n<li>Isothermal layer = temperature <strong>constant with height</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":4,"title":"Air Density","href":"chapters/met-04.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Higher density altitude = <strong>lower</strong> density.</li>\n<li>Pressure up (temperature constant) → density up → density altitude <strong>down</strong>.</li>\n<li>Less dense in high altitude, warm air <strong>and</strong> high humidity: \"<strong>all these</strong>\".</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":5,"title":"Humidity","href":"chapters/met-05.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Spread T − Td is <strong>least (zero)</strong> when saturated.</li>\n<li>\"Actual amount of WV in a given volume\" = <strong>absolute humidity</strong>.</li>\n<li>\"Lowest temperature by evaporating water into it\" = <strong>wet bulb</strong>, not dew point.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":6,"title":"Winds","href":"chapters/met-06.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Facing the wind in the SH, low is on your <strong>left</strong> (back to wind → right).</li>\n<li>Port drift (NH) → flying towards <strong>high</strong> → altimeter <strong>under-reads</strong>.</li>\n<li>Isotachs = equal wind speed.</li>\n<li>Sea breeze → temperature <strong>falls</strong>, RH <strong>rises</strong>.</li>\n<li>Thermal wind is parallel to <strong>isotherms</strong> (thickness lines), not isobars.</li>\n<li>Inertial flow: <strong>anticyclonic in both hemispheres</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":7,"title":"Visibility and Fog","href":"chapters/met-07.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Fog <strong>&lt; 1000 m</strong>; mist <strong>1000–5000 m</strong> (RH ~100%); haze <strong>≤ 5000 m</strong> (dry).</li>\n<li>Warm moist air over a cold surface = <strong>advection fog</strong> (fog and stratus).</li>\n<li>Radiation fog: <strong>land, night, clear sky, light wind, high RH, inversion, highs and cols</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fog is <strong>stratus</strong> on the ground. Measured by a <strong>transmissometer</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":8,"title":"Vertical Motion and Clouds","href":"chapters/met-08.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Drizzle → <strong>ST</strong>. Showers → <strong>CB</strong>. Continuous rain → <strong>NS</strong>. Heaviest icing → <strong>CB</strong>.</li>\n<li>Halo → <strong>CS</strong> (ice); corona → <strong>AS</strong> (book key).</li>\n<li>Feathery ice cloud → <strong>CI</strong>. Lenticular → <strong>mountain waves</strong>.</li>\n<li>Ceiling = <strong>≥ 5/8</strong> below 20,000 ft.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":9,"title":"Stability and Instability","href":"chapters/met-09.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>DALR ≈ <strong>10°C/km</strong> (9.8) = 3°C/1000 ft; SALR ≈ <strong>5°C/km</strong> at MSL.</li>\n<li>ELR &lt; SALR → <strong>absolutely stable</strong>; ELR &gt; DALR → <strong>absolutely unstable</strong>.</li>\n<li>Inversion = <strong>negative</strong> lapse rate = <strong>stability</strong>; common in <strong>winter</strong>.</li>\n<li>The process mainly fixing the vertical temperature distribution = <strong>adiabatic</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":10,"title":"Optical Phenomena","href":"chapters/met-10.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Halo = <strong>refraction</strong>, <strong>ice</strong>, <strong>CS</strong>, <strong>22°</strong>, <strong>red outside</strong>, <strong>negligible icing</strong>, <strong>high</strong> cloud.</li>\n<li>Corona = <strong>diffraction</strong>, <strong>AS</strong>, small radius, red outside.</li>\n<li>Superior mirage = <strong>inversion</strong>; inferior = <strong>steep lapse</strong>.</li>\n<li>Bishop's ring = <strong>dust</strong>, ~22°.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":12,"title":"Ice Accretion","href":"chapters/met-12.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Small drops → <strong>rime</strong>; large drops → <strong>glaze</strong>; clear-air deposition → <strong>hoar frost</strong>.</li>\n<li>Most dangerous = <strong>glaze (clear) ice</strong>. Severe band <strong>0 to −7°C</strong>.</li>\n<li>Carburettor icing possible at <strong>+30°C</strong>; unlikely below <strong>60% RH</strong>.</li>\n<li>Icing <strong>increases</strong> stall speed.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":13,"title":"Thunderstorms","href":"chapters/met-13.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Mature stage = <strong>up- AND downdraughts</strong>; cumulus = updraughts only; dissipating = downdraughts only.</li>\n<li>Norwesters: <strong>Mar–May, afternoons, Bengal/Bihar/Odisha/Assam, from Chota Nagpur</strong>.</li>\n<li>Microburst <strong>&lt; 4 km, 1–5 min</strong>; macroburst <strong>≥ 4 km</strong>.</li>\n<li>Lightning most likely <strong>+10 to −10°C</strong>. Hail grows by <strong>collision with supercooled drops</strong>.</li>\n<li>Altimeter <strong>over-reads</strong> under a CB.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":14,"title":"Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","href":"chapters/met-14.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Polar maritime = sea at high latitude; Tc = hot and dry; Em = hot and wet (SW monsoon).</li>\n<li>Line squalls form <strong>ahead</strong> of cold fronts; cold front moves at <strong>double</strong> a warm front's speed.</li>\n<li>FZRA/FZFG <strong>ahead</strong> of a warm front. Precipitation ceases after a <strong>warm</strong> front passes.</li>\n<li>WD = <strong>occluded front</strong>; maximum in <strong>winter</strong>; induced lows to the <strong>south</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":16,"title":"CAT and Mountain Waves","href":"chapters/met-16.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Jet core minimum <strong>60 kt</strong>; vertical shear <strong>5 m/s/km</strong>; STJ <strong>westerly</strong>, TEJ <strong>easterly</strong> at <strong>13°N, 15–16 km, Jun–Aug</strong>; STJ over India <strong>Oct–May</strong>, southernmost <strong>22°N in Feb</strong>.</li>\n<li>STJ strengthens <strong>downstream</strong>; vertical shear greater <strong>above</strong> the core.</li>\n<li>Recognise a jet by <strong>cirrus streaks</strong>. Strongest CAT: <strong>cold side, just below the axis</strong> (left looking downstream, NH).</li>\n<li>Mountain waves: <strong>30°</strong>, <strong>7 / 15 m/s</strong>, <strong>stable below</strong>; worst turbulence in <strong>rotor</strong> clouds.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":18,"title":"Tropical Systems","href":"chapters/met-18.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Eye = calm, clear, warm, lowest pressure; <strong>eye wall</strong> = most dangerous.</li>\n<li>TC needs SST <strong>&gt; 26°C</strong>, latitude <strong>5–15°/5–25°</strong>, low shear; none at equator.</li>\n<li>Monsoon depression: <strong>head Bay</strong>, worst <strong>SW sector</strong>, slopes <strong>SW</strong>, life <strong>3–5 days</strong>.</li>\n<li>Surge = <strong>same</strong> air mass, <strong>velocity convergence</strong>; PD = <strong>central peninsula, pre-monsoon</strong>.</li>\n<li>Indian cyclone maximum <strong>Oct–Nov</strong>; Bay 4.5 / year vs Arabian Sea 1.1.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":19,"title":"Climatology of India","href":"chapters/met-19.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Summer season = <strong>Mar–May</strong>; monsoon <strong>Jun–Sep</strong>; autumn/post-monsoon <strong>Oct–Nov</strong>.</li>\n<li>Break monsoon: trough on the <strong>foothills</strong>, <strong>pressure rises</strong>, rain on the foothills, strong winds E UP/Bihar.</li>\n<li>Monsoon low over <strong>Pakistan/Baluchistan</strong>; steep gradient on the <strong>west coast</strong>.</li>\n<li>Winter: westerlies aloft; monsoon: <strong>easterlies aloft</strong> (TEJ).</li>\n<li>El Niño = <strong>warm phase</strong> of ENSO → weaker monsoon. Maximum cyclones <strong>post-monsoon</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":20,"title":"General Circulation","href":"chapters/met-20.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Subtropical high ↔ <strong>subsidence</strong> ↔ <strong>deserts at 30°</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Horse latitudes 30–40°</strong> (calm high); <strong>doldrums</strong> = equatorial calm.</li>\n<li>Trade winds NE (NH) / SE (SH); upper troposphere <strong>zonal westerlies</strong>, but <strong>easterlies over the equator</strong>.</li>\n<li>Radiation <strong>surplus in the tropics</strong>, deficit at the poles.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":21,"title":"Met Services for Aviation","href":"chapters/met-21.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>TREND 2 h</strong>, <strong>SIGMET ≤ 4 h</strong> (VA/TC 12 h ahead, updated 6 h), <strong>aerodrome warning ≤ 4 h</strong>, <strong>SIGWX 24 h</strong>, <strong>local/area 12 h</strong>, TAF <strong>9–30 h</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>18 AMOs, 54 AMSs, 4 MWOs, 6 RMOs</strong>; <strong>Chennai</strong> compiles the national SIGWX.</li>\n<li>Area forecast <strong>100 NM</strong>, local <strong>50 NM</strong>. Wind warning <strong>30 kt</strong>, light aircraft <strong>17 kt</strong>, direction change <strong>45°</strong>. Wind shear warning <strong>up to 500 m</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>ISOL &lt; 50% · OCNL 50–75% · FRQ &gt; 75%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>CODAR</strong> = aircraft upper-air report; <strong>WINTEM</strong> = forecast wind/temp; <strong>ROBEX</strong> = outside India.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":22,"title":"Weather Radar and Met Satellites","href":"chapters/met-22.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>S-band (10 cm)</strong> = cyclone detection radar; <strong>X-band (3 cm)</strong> = airborne and storm detection.</li>\n<li><strong>PPI = bearing and range; RHI = height</strong>.</li>\n<li>Drizzle invisible (return ∝ D⁶); <strong>bright band</strong> at the melting level = stratiform/weak storm; <strong>hook/figure-6</strong> = hail/tornado.</li>\n<li>Airborne colours Green &lt; Yellow &lt; Red &lt; Magenta. Geostationary <strong>36 000 km</strong>, polar <strong>650–1500 km</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":23,"title":"Met Instruments","href":"chapters/met-23.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Maximum thermometer = mercury</strong>, <strong>minimum = alcohol</strong>. Self-recording: baro<strong>graph</strong>, hygro<strong>graph</strong>, anemo<strong>graph</strong>, ceilo<strong>graph</strong>, hyeto<strong>graph</strong> (rain).</li>\n<li>Surface wind direction is from <strong>True</strong> north. <strong>Nephoscope</strong> gives cloud <strong>movement</strong>, not base. <strong>Dropsonde</strong> is dropped from aircraft (cyclones).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":25,"title":"METAR, SPECI and TREND","href":"chapters/met-25.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>QNH rounds <strong>down</strong>, temperature x.5 rounds <strong>up</strong>. Q1003 = 1003.0–1003.9; 32 = 31.5–32.4.</li>\n<li>VRB &lt; 3 kt; extreme directions only 60–180° and ≥ 3 kt; P99KT for ≥ 100 kt.</li>\n<li>Visibility steps 50/100/1000 m; 9999 ≥ 10 km. FG &lt; 1000, BR 1000–5000.</li>\n<li>CAVOK = all three conditions. TREND 2 h, TEMPO &lt; 1 h each.</li>\n<li>M0150 = below what the instrument can measure; P1500 = above.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":26,"title":"TAF, ARFOR and ROFOR","href":"chapters/met-26.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>TAF 9–30 h; 9-h TAF domestic, every 3 h; 12–30 h every 6 h. Validity group = <strong>date+hour/date+hour</strong>.</li>\n<li>Issue time in IST = UTC + 5 h 30 min.</li>\n<li>Indicator numbers 7/6/5/4/2 and 11111 = jet, 22222 = max wind.</li>\n<li>In figure codes heights are in <strong>hundreds of feet</strong> (020 = 2000 ft; 380 = 38 000 ft).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":27,"title":"Radar Report, SIGMET and Satellite Bulletin","href":"chapters/met-27.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>RAREP range in <strong>km</strong>, echo speed in <strong>km/h</strong>, top heights in <strong>km AMSL</strong>, heights reported only within 200 km.</li>\n<li>Squall line echo ≥ 60 km, 10:1. SLD full, BRKN 4–7/8, SCT 1–4/8, WDLY SCT &lt; 1/8.</li>\n<li>SIGMET SST for supersonic; AFTN DD priority; 1100 NM.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":29,"title":"Flight Forecast: Tabular and Cross-Section","href":"chapters/met-29.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>T-3 tabular ≤ 500 NM</strong>, <strong>T-4 cross-section &gt; 500 NM</strong>.</li>\n<li>T-3 heights in <strong>ft AMSL</strong>; T-4 in <strong>flight levels</strong>. Surface visibility only for flights below FL100.</li>\n</ul>"}],"styles":{"concept":277,"numeric":183,"fill":73,"definition":37,"cause":23,"code":36,"tf":21,"not":7},"n":657,"ex":{"concept":{"q":"Lowest layer of atmosphere is","o":["Troposphere","Tropopause","Stratosphere"],"a":0,"ch":1},"numeric":{"q":"SALR at mean sea level is about","o":["10°C/km","5°C/km","5°F/km"],"a":1,"ch":9},"fill":{"q":"Inversion is … lapse rate (ELR)","o":["Positive","Negative","Neutral"],"a":1,"ch":9},"definition":{"q":"CB with distinct anvil is called","o":["Castellanus","Capillatus","Uncinus"],"a":1,"ch":8},"cause":{"q":"Corona are formed due to the … of light","o":["Refraction","Diffraction","Scattering"],"a":1,"ch":10},"code":{"q":"The TAF has been issued on … day","o":["23rd","18th","24th"],"a":2,"ch":26},"tf":{"q":"Local winds follow Buys Ballot's law …","o":["False","True"],"a":0,"ch":6},"not":{"q":"Rain falling from cloud but not reaching ground is","o":["Virgo","Virga","Mirage"],"a":1,"ch":8}}},"nav":{"name":"Air Navigation","traps":[{"n":1,"title":"The Solar System","href":"chapters/nav-01.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Seasons ← <strong>tilt</strong>, not distance. Different day/night lengths ← <strong>inclination of the ecliptic to the equator</strong>.</li>\n<li>Perihelion <strong>January</strong>, aphelion <strong>July</strong>. Civil twilight = <strong>6°</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":2,"title":"The Earth","href":"chapters/nav-02.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Ellipticity 1/297: 6956 km equatorial axis → polar ≈ 6932 km.</li>\n<li>Max geocentric–geodetic difference at <strong>45°</strong>.</li>\n<li>NM longest at the <strong>poles</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":4,"title":"Convergency","href":"chapters/nav-04.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Formula is <strong>d.long × sin mean lat</strong> (not d.lat, not cos).</li>\n<li>CA is <strong>half</strong> the convergency.</li>\n<li>60°N, 120°W → 140°30′W: RL 270°, convergency ≈ 17.8°, initial GC ≈ 279°.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":5,"title":"Time","href":"chapters/nav-05.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Always convert to UTC first. Watch the date when crossing midnight or the date line.</li>\n<li>Same latitude → same LMT of sunrise/sunset.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":6,"title":"Compass and Directions","href":"chapters/nav-06.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>When variation is east, the magnetic direction is <strong>less</strong> than the true direction.</li>\n<li>Agonic = zero variation; aclinic = zero dip (next chapter).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":7,"title":"Distances on the Earth","href":"chapters/nav-07.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Convert km → NM (÷ 1.852) before converting to degrees of latitude.</li>\n<li>Watch for crossing 180° when adding d.long.</li>\n<li>1° of latitude is always 60 NM; 1° of longitude is 60 NM only on the equator.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":8,"title":"Magnetism and Compasses","href":"chapters/nav-08.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Coefficient <strong>B on E/W</strong>, <strong>C on N/S</strong>. Correct B on east, C on north.</li>\n<li>APDE and \"sluggish through the pole\".</li>\n<li>Dip zero at the magnetic equator; H max there.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":9,"title":"Dead Reckoning","href":"chapters/nav-09.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Starboard drift is <strong>subtracted</strong> from track to get heading; port drift added.</li>\n<li>Convert true wind to magnetic before runway components.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":10,"title":"DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","href":"chapters/nav-10.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>QNH uses <strong>ISA</strong>; QFF uses actual temperature.</li>\n<li>DA rises with temperature; TA = PA corrected by 4%/10 °C.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":11,"title":"DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","href":"chapters/nav-11.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>TAT &gt; SAT always; difference ∝ TAS (not CAS).</li>\n<li>Use kelvin in the TAT formula.</li>\n<li>In an isothermal layer constant Mach ⇔ constant TAS.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":12,"title":"PNR, PET and Range","href":"chapters/nav-12.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>CP formula uses <strong>D</strong>, PNR formula uses <strong>E</strong> (endurance) — both × H/(O + H).</li>\n<li>Time to CP uses the normal all-engines GS.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":13,"title":"In-flight Navigation","href":"chapters/nav-13.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>QTE from an NDB uses <strong>variation at the aircraft</strong>; from a VOR, <strong>variation at the station</strong>.</li>\n<li>Revised ETAs: use the GS actually achieved on the last leg.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":14,"title":"Mass and Balance","href":"chapters/nav-14.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>ZFM and DOM differ by the <strong>traffic load</strong>; useful load = traffic load + usable fuel.</li>\n<li>CG shift = mass moved × distance ÷ total mass.</li>\n<li>Fuel in litres entered as kg makes the aircraft lighter than calculated (density &lt; 1).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":15,"title":"Performance","href":"chapters/nav-15.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Stopway → ASDA only; clearway → TODA only; displaced threshold → LDA only.</li>\n<li>The <strong>second segment</strong> limits performance.</li>\n<li>VAT = 1.3 VSO (100 kt stall → 130 kt).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":16,"title":"Flight Planning and Monitoring","href":"chapters/nav-16.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Submit <strong>≥ 60 min</strong> before departure, <strong>≤ 120 h</strong> before EOBT.</li>\n<li>Delay <strong>30 min</strong> (controlled) / <strong>1 h</strong> (uncontrolled) → amend or refile.</li>\n<li>Report TAS change <strong>± 10 kt / M0.02</strong>; time change <strong>&gt; 2 min</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Y</strong> = IFR first; <strong>Z</strong> = VFR first.</li>\n<li><strong>HEAVY ≥ 136 t; LIGHT ≤ 7 t.</strong></li>\n<li>Max <strong>2</strong> destination alternates; Item 7 max <strong>7</strong> characters.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":17,"title":"Air Data Systems","href":"chapters/nav-17.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Pitot</strong> feeds the <strong>ASI only</strong>; <strong>static</strong> feeds <strong>ASI, altimeter and VSI</strong>.</li>\n<li>Alternate static inside the cabin: <strong>all three over-read</strong> (VSI a brief climb).</li>\n<li>Compressibility → ASI <strong>over-reads</strong>; ignore below <strong>300 kt TAS</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":18,"title":"Altimeter","href":"chapters/nav-18.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>High to low (pressure or temperature): look out below</strong> — the altimeter over-reads.</li>\n<li>Servo: <strong>induction pick-off</strong>, <strong>± 60 ft</strong> at sea level, lag removed.</li>\n<li>Vibrator: reduces <strong>friction / instrument error</strong>, not hysteresis.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":19,"title":"Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","href":"chapters/nav-19.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>ASI: <strong>pitot in the capsule, static in the case</strong>; measures <strong>P − S</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SOD SUC PUD POC</strong>; pitot + drain blocked → behaves <strong>like an altimeter</strong>.</li>\n<li>White <strong>VS0–VFE</strong>, green <strong>VS1–VNO</strong>, yellow <strong>VNO–VNE</strong>, red <strong>VNE</strong>, blue <strong>VYSE</strong>.</li>\n<li>Compressibility → <strong>over-reads</strong> above <strong>300 kt TAS</strong>; correction negative → EAS.</li>\n<li>Machmeter = <strong>(P − S) ÷ S</strong>; <strong>no temperature or density error</strong>; LSS depends on <strong>temperature only</strong>.</li>\n<li>High level limited by <strong>MMO</strong>, low level by <strong>VMO</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":20,"title":"Vertical Speed Indicator","href":"chapters/nav-20.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Capsule <strong>direct static</strong>, case <strong>static through a calibrated leak</strong>; climb → capsule <strong>contracts</strong>.</li>\n<li>Static blocked → <strong>zero</strong>. Lag is the main error; <strong>IVSI</strong> removes it with <strong>dashpots</strong> but shows a <strong>climb in a steep turn</strong>.</li>\n<li>Pressurised-aircraft case leak → <strong>high in climb, low in descent</strong>; unpressurised → <strong>zero</strong>.</li>\n<li>Transonic jump = brief <strong>false</strong> reading near Mach 1.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":21,"title":"Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","href":"chapters/nav-21.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Rigidity ↑ with <strong>RPM, mass, radius</strong>; precession rate ∝ <strong>force ÷ RPM</strong>, 90° on in the direction of rotation.</li>\n<li><strong>AH = vertical earth gyro</strong>; <strong>DGI = horizontal tied gyro, 2 gimbals</strong>; <strong>TSI = horizontal rate gyro, 1 degree of freedom</strong>.</li>\n<li>Air-driven AH: <strong>acceleration → climb + right bank</strong>; turns → <strong>climb</strong>; 90° under-bank, 270° over-bank, 360° correct.</li>\n<li>DGI drift <strong>15 sin lat °/h</strong>; latitude nut <strong>SONI</strong>; check against the compass <strong>every 15 min</strong>.</li>\n<li>Rate 1 = <strong>3°/s</strong>, bank ≈ <strong>TAS/10 + 7</strong>. <strong>ROGOTU</strong>. Ball <strong>in = slip</strong>, <strong>out = skid</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":22,"title":"Inertial Navigation","href":"chapters/nav-22.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Integrate <strong>once → velocity, twice → distance</strong>. Sensors measure <strong>acceleration</strong>.</li>\n<li>Align <strong>stationary</strong>; NAV before moving; lost in flight → <strong>ATT mode</strong> (attitude, rates, acceleration only).</li>\n<li>TAS input is only for <strong>wind</strong>. Schuler <strong>84.4 min</strong>, bounded.</li>\n<li>RLG: <strong>dither motor</strong> against <strong>laser lock</strong>; no precession or topple; fast alignment.</li>\n<li>Error rate = <strong>radial error ÷ hours in NAV</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":23,"title":"Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","href":"chapters/nav-23.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>RADALT: <strong>FMCW 4200–4400 MHz (SHF, centimetric)</strong>, two aerials, <strong>frequency difference ∝ height</strong>, no Doppler, 0–2500 ft, ±2 ft to 500 ft. Gear up → <strong>under-reads</strong>. Tested on the ground only.</li>\n<li>TCAS: TA = information; <strong>RA = act</strong>, vertical only in ACAS II; \"Monitor/maintain vertical speed\" = <strong>preventive</strong> RA. RA against any <strong>mode C/S</strong> transponder.</li>\n<li>GPWS modes: <strong>1 sink rate, 2 terrain closure, 3 don't sink, 4 too low gear/flaps, 5 glideslope, 6 minimums/bank, 7 windshear</strong>. Range <strong>50–2450 ft</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":24,"title":"Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","href":"chapters/nav-24.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Bourdon tube = pressure</strong> (higher pressures); capsule/bellows for low pressure.</li>\n<li>Temperature methods: <strong>thermocouple, resistance, liquid (mercury)</strong> — not reactance.</li>\n<li><strong>Thermocouple needs no power</strong>; oil temperature uses <strong>resistance or vapour pressure</strong>.</li>\n<li>CAT gauge: <strong>yellow −10 to +15 °C icing</strong>, red above +40 °C detonation.</li>\n<li>Capacitance gauge → <strong>mass</strong>; water makes it over-read.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":25,"title":"Basics of Electronic Displays","href":"chapters/nav-25.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>PFD = <strong>piloting</strong>; ND = navigation. Boeing <strong>EADI + EHSI</strong>, Airbus <strong>PFD + ND</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Magenta = active</strong>, cyan = inactive, amber = caution, red = warning.</li>\n<li>DH set on the <strong>EFIS control panel (ADI section)</strong>, flashes <strong>yellow at DH</strong>; RA circular below <strong>1000 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li>Magnetic north between <strong>73°N and 65°S</strong>.</li>\n<li>Weather radar <strong>not in PLAN</strong> (only MAP and expanded VOR/ILS). Distance to go <strong>top left</strong>.</li>\n<li>EICAS: <strong>upper primary, lower secondary</strong>; maintenance mode ground only. ECAM: checklist left, diagram right.</li>\n<li>Nav database <strong>28 days</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":26,"title":"Basic Radio Theory","href":"chapters/nav-26.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>λ = c/f: <strong>12 mm → 25 GHz</strong>, <strong>3.5 cm → 8.57 GHz</strong>, <strong>19 cm → 1.579 GHz (UHF)</strong>, <strong>121.95 MHz → 2.46 m</strong>, <strong>373 kHz → about 800 m</strong>.</li>\n<li>VOR, VHF and ILS localiser are <strong>metric</strong>; weather radar is <strong>centimetric</strong>; NDB is <strong>kilometric/hectometric</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>295 kHz is LF</strong> (LF ends at 300 kHz), not MF.</li>\n<li>Skip distance is <strong>greater by night</strong>; HF frequency must be <strong>decreased at night</strong>.</li>\n<li>Dead space is <strong>between</strong> the end of the surface wave and the first sky wave; skip distance is measured <strong>from the transmitter</strong>.</li>\n<li>Ducting: <strong>inversion + humidity decreasing with height</strong>.</li>\n<li>The book's grid gives \"refraction increases with frequency\"; physically refraction <strong>decreases</strong> as frequency rises (that is why skip distance grows) — Glidepath's bank follows the physics and notes the book key.</li>\n</ul>"}],"styles":{"concept":493,"cause":32,"numeric":298,"definition":21,"not":18,"code":55,"fill":17},"n":934,"ex":{"concept":{"q":"In an ortho morphic projection,","o":["Meridians and parallels intersect at right angles","Meridians and parallels are straight lines","Both the above"],"a":0,"ch":3},"cause":{"q":"Seasons are due to the:","o":["Earth's elliptical orbit around the Sun","inclination of the polar axis with the ecliptic plane","Earth's rotation on its polar axis"],"a":1,"ch":1},"numeric":{"q":"A flight plan can be submitted:","o":["30 days before EOBT","24 hrs before ETD","120 hrs before EOBT"],"a":2,"ch":16},"definition":{"q":"A radio altimeter can be defined as a?","o":["Ground radio aid used to measure the true height of the aircraft.","Ground radio aid used to measure the true altitude of the aircraft.","Self-contained on-board aid used to measure the true height of the aircraft"],"a":2,"ch":23},"not":{"q":"Parallels of latitude, except the equator, are?","o":["Rhumb lines.","Complex curves.","Great circle."],"a":0,"ch":3},"code":{"q":"A white arc on an ASI indicates:","o":["flaps extension speed range","safe cruise speed","safe speed on approach to land"],"a":0,"ch":19},"fill":{"q":"VNO is … and is indicated by the … On an ASI?","o":["Min speed for normal operations Lower end of green arc.","Max speed for normal operations upper end of green arc.","Min speed for night operations lower end of white are."],"a":1,"ch":19}}},"reg":{"name":"Air Regulations & Human Factors","traps":[{"n":1,"title":"Definitions and Abbreviations","href":"chapters/reg-01.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Movement area includes aprons; manoeuvring area does not.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>TODA adds clearway; ASDA adds stopway.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>CTR starts at the surface; CTA starts above the surface.</strong></li>\n<li>MSA = <strong>1,000 ft within 25 NM</strong>; ceiling = <strong>below 20,000 ft, more than half the sky</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cat I <strong>200 ft / 550 m RVR</strong>; Cat II <strong>100 ft / 300 m</strong>; Cat III <strong>below 100 ft / below 300 m</strong>.</li>\n<li>Small aeroplane is <strong>5,700 kg or less</strong> (exactly 5,700 kg is small).</li>\n<li>Wet runway: up to and <strong>including 3 mm</strong>.</li>\n<li>DH is referenced to the <strong>threshold</strong>; MDH to the <strong>aerodrome</strong> (or threshold if more than 7 ft lower).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":2,"title":"International Organisations and Conventions","href":"chapters/reg-02.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Over the <strong>high seas</strong>: rules <strong>established under the Convention</strong> (Annex 2), not those of any State.</li>\n<li>Difference from a Standard: <strong>immediate</strong> notice; from an <strong>amended</strong> Standard: within <strong>60 days</strong>.</li>\n<li>Annexes with <strong>Standards only: 2, 5, 7, 8</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cabotage</strong> = traffic between two points in the same foreign State (Art 7).</li>\n<li>5th freedom involves <strong>two other States</strong>. India ratified the <strong>Transit</strong> agreement (freedoms 1–2), not the Transport agreement.</li>\n<li>Tokyo: in flight for jurisdiction = take-off power to end of landing run; for commander's powers = <strong>doors closed to doors open</strong>.</li>\n<li>Council = <strong>36</strong>; ANC = <strong>19</strong>; Assembly at least every <strong>3 years</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":3,"title":"Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","href":"chapters/reg-03.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Fuselage/tail marks <strong>30 cm</strong>; wings and lighter-than-air <strong>50 cm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Registration mark assigned by the <strong>State of Registry or common mark registering authority</strong> (not ITU, not ICAO).</li>\n<li>Common mark assigned by <strong>ICAO</strong>; symbols allotted to ICAO by the <strong>ITU</strong>.</li>\n<li>Avoid confusion with <strong>XXX, PAN, TTT, SOS</strong>, 3-letter <strong>Q</strong> groups and 5-letter code-of-signals groups.</li>\n<li>India: <strong>VT</strong> civil, <strong>VU</strong> Air Force; ITU block <strong>AT–AW</strong> unused.</li>\n<li>Registration cancelled if C of A expired <strong>5 years or more</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":4,"title":"Rules of the Air","href":"chapters/reg-04.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Head-on: <strong>both turn right</strong>. Converging: the one with the other <strong>on its right</strong> gives way. Overtaking: overtaker turns <strong>right</strong>; within <strong>70°</strong> of the tail.</li>\n<li>Powered aircraft give way to <strong>airships, gliders, balloons</strong>; gliders give way only to <strong>balloons</strong>.</li>\n<li>Of two landing aircraft, the <strong>lower</strong> has priority, but powered aircraft still give way to <strong>gliders</strong>.</li>\n<li>Flight plan <strong>≥ 3 h before</strong>, <strong>≤ 120 h before</strong> EOBT; delay <strong>15 min</strong> → tell ATC.</li>\n<li>RCF: <strong>20 min</strong> (non-radar) / <strong>7 min</strong> (radar); land within <strong>30 min</strong> of ETA/EAT.</li>\n<li>VMC: <strong>8 km</strong> at and above 10,000 ft, else <strong>5 km</strong>; <strong>1,500 m / 1,000 ft</strong> from cloud; F/G low: clear of cloud, surface in sight.</li>\n<li>Steady red in flight = <strong>give way and keep circling</strong>; red flashes in flight = <strong>aerodrome unsafe</strong>; red pyro = <strong>do not land for the time being</strong>.</li>\n<li>Two yellow diagonals = <strong>landing prohibited</strong>; one diagonal = <strong>special precautions</strong>.</li>\n<li>Aerobatics must <strong>finish by 2,000 ft AGL</strong>; not within <strong>2 NM</strong> of an aerodrome below <strong>6,000 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li>Interception: follow <strong>visual</strong> signals over other radio instructions; squawk <strong>7700</strong>; 121.5 MHz.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":5,"title":"Air Traffic Services","href":"chapters/reg-05.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Aerodrome</strong> control achieves objectives <strong>a, b and c</strong>; area and approach achieve <strong>a and c</strong> only.</li>\n<li>Class <strong>B</strong>: VFR separated from <strong>everyone</strong>. Class <strong>C</strong>: VFR separated only from <strong>IFR</strong>. Class <strong>D</strong>: VFR gets <strong>no separation</strong>. Class <strong>E</strong>: VFR needs <strong>no clearance or radio</strong>. Class <strong>F</strong>: <strong>advisory</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>250 kt below 10,000 ft</strong> applies to C(VFR), D, E, F, G — not A, B or IFR in C.</li>\n<li>CTR at least <strong>5 NM</strong>; CTA base at least <strong>700 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li>INCERFA <strong>30 min</strong>; ALERFA <strong>5 min</strong> after landing clearance; <strong>7500</strong> hijack, <strong>7600</strong> radio, <strong>7700</strong> emergency.</li>\n<li>Read back <strong>SSR codes, altimeter settings, levels, headings, speeds, runway clearances, TL</strong>; not CPDLC.</li>\n<li><strong>ILS</strong> voice channel never used for ATIS. ATIS ≤ <strong>30 s</strong>.</li>\n<li>India border buffer <strong>15 NM</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":6,"title":"Separation Methods and Minima","href":"chapters/reg-06.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Vertical: <strong>1,000 ft below FL 290, 2,000 ft above</strong>, RVSM <strong>1,000 ft</strong> to FL 410.</li>\n<li>VOR <strong>15° and 15 NM</strong>; NDB <strong>30° and 15 NM</strong>.</li>\n<li>Same track <strong>&lt; 45°</strong>; reciprocal <strong>135–225°</strong>.</li>\n<li>5 min needs <strong>20 kt</strong>; 3 min needs <strong>40 kt</strong>; DME 10 NM needs <strong>20 kt</strong>.</li>\n<li>Reciprocal: <strong>10 min</strong> either side; DME: <strong>10 NM</strong> after passing.</li>\n<li>India radar <strong>5 NM</strong> (3 NM at five big radars). No <strong>cruise climb</strong> in India.</li>\n<li>MNT <strong>10 min</strong> at <strong>M 0.02</strong>; RNAV + MNT <strong>80 NM</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":7,"title":"Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","href":"chapters/reg-07.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Departures: <strong>1 min / 45°</strong>, <strong>2 min / 40 kt same track</strong>, <strong>5 min</strong> climbing through.</li>\n<li>Arrival on full procedure: departures any direction <strong>until procedure/base turn starts</strong>, then <strong>45° off the reciprocal and 3 min before</strong>.</li>\n<li>Straight-in: any direction until <strong>5 min</strong> before; 45° off until <strong>3 min</strong> before.</li>\n<li>Visual approach visibility: <strong>2,800 / 3,200 / 3,600 m</strong> (A-B / C / D); circling-only runway <strong>5 km</strong>.</li>\n<li>EAT if delay <strong>≥ 10 min</strong>; revise if change <strong>≥ 5 min</strong>; quickest means if holding <strong>≥ 30 min</strong>.</li>\n<li>Significant wind change components: <strong>10 kt head, 2 kt tail, 5 kt cross</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":8,"title":"Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","href":"chapters/reg-08.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Ground controller: manoeuvring area <strong>except runways</strong>.</li>\n<li>Taxi limit beyond a runway → <strong>explicit cross or hold short</strong>.</li>\n<li>\"Runway vacated\" = <strong>whole aircraft beyond the holding position</strong>.</li>\n<li>Holding without a marked position: <strong>50 m</strong> (runway ≥ 900 m) / <strong>30 m</strong> (&lt; 900 m) from the edge.</li>\n<li>Before taxi info order: <strong>runway, wind, QNH, temperature, visibility (&lt; 10 km), time</strong>.</li>\n<li>Lost on a runway: <strong>tell ATS, vacate, then stop</strong>; lost elsewhere: <strong>stop and tell</strong>.</li>\n<li>Lights by day if visibility <strong>≤ 5,000 m</strong>; back on <strong>1 h</strong> before arrival; runway lit <strong>15 min</strong> after departure.</li>\n<li>PAPI on <strong>day and night</strong> when the runway is used.</li>\n<li>Friction <strong>0.40+ good, 0.25− poor</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":9,"title":"Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","href":"chapters/reg-09.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>ADS-B <strong>broadcasts to all</strong>; ADS-C reports <strong>by contract</strong> to specific units.</li>\n<li>MLAT uses <strong>TDOA</strong>, needs <strong>no new avionics</strong>.</li>\n<li>Radar advisory = <strong>Class F</strong>; radar FIS = anywhere in the FIR.</li>\n<li>Departing-aircraft identification within <strong>1 NM</strong> of runway end; turn method <strong>≥ 30°</strong>.</li>\n<li>ICAO <strong>5 / 3 / 2.5 NM</strong> (2.5 within 10 NM on the same final).</li>\n<li>Speed: <strong>250 kt below FL 100</strong>, <strong>220 kt within 15 NM</strong> for arrivals.</li>\n<li>Mode C tolerance <strong>±200 ft RVSM</strong>, <strong>±300 ft</strong> elsewhere. No code assigned → <strong>2000</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":10,"title":"Aeronautical Information Services","href":"chapters/reg-10.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>AIS by <strong>AAI</strong>; AIC by <strong>DGCA</strong>.</li>\n<li>Permanent change → <strong>AIP AMDT</strong>; temporary <strong>≥ 3 months</strong> or bulky → <strong>AIP SUP (yellow)</strong>; short temporary → <strong>NOTAM</strong>; neither → <strong>AIC</strong>.</li>\n<li>AIRAC cycle <strong>28 days</strong>; distribute <strong>42 days</strong> ahead to arrive <strong>28 days</strong> ahead; major changes <strong>56 days</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>7 days'</strong> notice to activate P/R/D areas.</li>\n<li>NOTAM checklist <strong>monthly</strong>; AIC checklist <strong>yearly</strong>; SUP checklist <strong>monthly</strong> (India: AIP SUP checklist each <strong>January</strong>).</li>\n<li>India NOTAM series: A &gt; 2 h international; B 30 min–2 h; C domestic; D military; G general (Delhi only).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":11,"title":"Search and Rescue","href":"chapters/reg-11.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>RCC and RSC organise SAR; SRRs <strong>do not overlap</strong>.</li>\n<li>India: AAI's <strong>4 RCCs</strong> (Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai); Coast Guard's <strong>3 MRCCs</strong> (Mumbai, Chennai, <strong>Port Blair</strong>). Land SAR chaired by <strong>Secretary MoCA</strong>; maritime by <strong>DG Coast Guard</strong>.</li>\n<li>First non-SAR aircraft on scene <strong>takes charge</strong> until a SAR aircraft arrives.</li>\n<li>Direct a ship: <strong>circle, cross ahead rocking wings, head off</strong>. Not needed: <strong>cross astern</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>V</strong> assistance, <strong>X</strong> medical, <strong>N</strong> no, <strong>Y</strong> yes; <strong>LLL</strong> completed, <strong>XX</strong> returning.</li>\n<li>Blue streamers = <strong>food and water</strong>. Symbols ≥ <strong>2.5 m</strong>. COSPAS-SARSAT <strong>406 MHz</strong>, <strong>5 km</strong>, INMCC <strong>Bengaluru</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":12,"title":"Visual Aids for Navigation","href":"chapters/reg-12.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Runway markings <strong>white</strong>; taxiway <strong>yellow</strong>; apron safety lines <strong>red (India)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Threshold stripes: <strong>18→4, 23→6, 30→8, 45→12, 60→16</strong>; start <strong>6 m</strong> in.</li>\n<li>Aiming point <strong>150/250/300/400 m</strong>; TDZ pairs <strong>1/2/3/4/6</strong>.</li>\n<li>Simple ALS <strong>420 m</strong>, crossbar at <strong>300 m</strong>; Cat I <strong>900 m</strong>, 30 m crossbar at 300 m; Cat II/III side rows <strong>270 m</strong> red, crossbars <strong>150 m and 300 m</strong>.</li>\n<li>PAPI <strong>2 red 2 white = on slope</strong>; <strong>4 units</strong>, left side; APAPI <strong>2 units</strong>.</li>\n<li>Edge lights <strong>white</strong>, <strong>yellow</strong> last 600 m / one-third; threshold <strong>green</strong>; end <strong>red</strong>; centre line <strong>white → red/white at 900 m → red last 300 m</strong>; taxiway centre <strong>green</strong>, edge <strong>blue</strong>; exit taxiway <strong>green/yellow</strong>.</li>\n<li>Stop bars for <strong>RVR &lt; 350 m</strong>; runway guard lights <strong>&lt; 550 m</strong> (or 550–1,200 m heavy traffic).</li>\n<li>Mandatory signs <strong>white on red</strong>; location sign <strong>yellow on black</strong>; other information signs <strong>black on yellow</strong>. No <strong>I, O, X</strong> for taxiways.</li>\n<li>Beacon <strong>20–30 flashes/min</strong>, green (land) / yellow (water).</li>\n<li>RFF response <strong>3 min</strong> (objective 2); foam principal; <strong>700 movements</strong> rule.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":13,"title":"PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","href":"chapters/reg-13.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>PDG <strong>3.3% = 2.5% OIS + 0.8%</strong>; MOC <strong>0</strong> at DER growing <strong>0.8%</strong>; turns not before <strong>394 ft</strong> and <strong>600 m</strong> from the runway start.</li>\n<li>Straight departure <strong>≤ 15°</strong>; straight-in NPA <strong>≤ 30°</strong>.</li>\n<li>Categories by <strong>Vat</strong>: <strong>91 / 121 / 141 / 166 / 211 kt</strong> boundaries; circling <strong>100/135/180/205/240</strong>.</li>\n<li>Final descent <strong>5.2% (3°) optimum</strong>, max <strong>6.5% A–B</strong>, <strong>6.1% C–E</strong>.</li>\n<li>MOC: initial <strong>1,000 ft</strong>, intermediate <strong>1,000 → 500 ft</strong>, final NPA <strong>246 ft with FAF / 295 ft without</strong>, missed approach <strong>164 ft</strong>, holding <strong>984 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li>Missed approach gradient <strong>2.5%</strong>; no turns in the initial phase; ≤ 15° in intermediate.</li>\n<li>Holding: <strong>230 / 240 / 265 kt / M 0.83</strong>; outbound <strong>1 min ≤ 14,000 ft</strong>, <strong>1.5 min</strong> above; teardrop <strong>30°</strong>.</li>\n<li>Parallel runways <strong>1,035 / 915 / 760 / 760 m</strong>.</li>\n<li>TA in India ≥ <strong>4,000 ft</strong>; altimeter check <strong>± 60 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li>RA beats ATC; TA alone → no manoeuvre; RAs only in the <strong>vertical</strong> plane.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":14,"title":"National Law","href":"chapters/reg-14.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Three bodies: <strong>DGCA safety, BCAS security, AAIB investigation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Penalty <strong>2 years / ₹1 crore</strong>; slaughter/waste within <strong>10 km</strong>: <strong>3 years</strong>; buildings/trees <strong>20 km</strong>.</li>\n<li>Alcohol <strong>12 hours</strong>; minimum solo age <strong>16</strong>; max <strong>65</strong> (60–65 multi-crew with a pilot under 60).</li>\n<li>Renewal: <strong>&lt; 2 y</strong> normal, <strong>2–3 y</strong> skill test + Air Regs, <strong>&gt; 3 y</strong> everything. Sick <strong>15 days</strong> → new medical.</li>\n<li>Documents not on board: produce in <strong>7 days</strong>. Logbooks kept <strong>5 years</strong>, certified <strong>quarterly</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fuelling: <strong>30 m</strong> no smoking, <strong>3 m</strong> danger zone, <strong>15 m / 6 m</strong> equipment, <strong>15 m</strong> people, <strong>43 m</strong> jet exhaust, <strong>900 L</strong> bulk.</li>\n<li>Cabin crew: <strong>1</strong> for 10–50 seats, <strong>2</strong> above 50, +1 per 50 above 99.</li>\n<li>Category <strong>A</strong> registration for public transport/aerial work.</li>\n<li>Health report <strong>2 h</strong> before landing; yellow fever <strong>6 days</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":15,"title":"Personnel Licensing","href":"chapters/reg-15.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Cross-country <strong>100 NM</strong> (PPL/microlight <strong>50 NM</strong>).</li>\n<li>ELP <strong>4 → 3 years, 5 → 6 years, 6 → never</strong>.</li>\n<li>Class 1: <strong>12 months to 60, 6 after</strong> (single-crew &gt; 40: 6 months); Class 2: <strong>24 months to 50, 12 after</strong>; Class 3: <strong>4/2/1 years</strong> at 40/50.</li>\n<li>Licences now valid <strong>10 years</strong>.</li>\n<li>Ages <strong>16 / 17 / 18 / 21</strong>.</li>\n<li>PPL <strong>40 h (20 solo, 150 NM XC)</strong>; CPL <strong>200 h (100 PIC, 300 NM XC, 10 h IF, 5 h night)</strong>; ATPL <strong>1,500 h (500 XC, 75 night, 100 IF)</strong>.</li>\n<li>CPL PIC limit <strong>5,700 kg</strong>; ATPL PIC &gt; 5,700 kg after <strong>100 h co-pilot + 10 route checks (5 night)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Night passengers: PPL <strong>5</strong>, CPL <strong>10</strong> take-offs and landings in 6 months.</li>\n<li>IR <strong>12 months</strong>; proficiency check <strong>6 months</strong>; renew within <strong>2 months</strong> of expiry to keep the date.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":16,"title":"Airworthiness of Aircraft","href":"chapters/reg-16.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Type Certificate → State of Design</strong>; <strong>C of A → State of Registry</strong> (DGCA for VT aircraft).</li>\n<li>Annex 8 = engineering; Annex 6 = operational.</li>\n<li>Annex 8 large-aeroplane standards: <strong>multi-engined, &gt; 5,700 kg</strong>.</li>\n<li>Major damage or defect → C of A <strong>deemed suspended</strong> → only a <strong>special flight permit</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":17,"title":"Operational Procedures","href":"chapters/reg-17.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Annex 6 Part II from <strong>1969</strong>, Part III <strong>1986</strong>.</li>\n<li>Flight deck admission: <strong>commander decides</strong>.</li>\n<li>LVTO = <strong>RVR &lt; 400 m</strong>; scheduled down to <strong>125 m</strong> (90 m visual segment); GA/non-scheduled <strong>500 m</strong>.</li>\n<li>Restricted AOM <strong>+100 ft, +400 m</strong>. Approach ban: <strong>1,000 ft</strong> or the FAF.</li>\n<li>TDZ RVR always controlling; mid-point <strong>125 m</strong>, stop-end <strong>50 m</strong>.</li>\n<li>Contingency fuel <strong>5%</strong> (min 5 min hold at 1,500 ft); final reserve <strong>turbine 30 min</strong>, piston <strong>45 min</strong>; no-alternate <strong>15 min</strong>; isolated turbine <strong>2 h</strong>.</li>\n<li>FDR <strong>25 h</strong>, CVR <strong>2 h</strong> (new &gt; 27 t: <strong>25 h</strong>). Two combination recorders &gt; <strong>15,000 kg</strong>.</li>\n<li>GPWS/wind shear: <strong>&gt; 5,700 kg or &gt; 9 pax</strong>; ACAS II: <strong>&gt; 5,700 kg or &gt; 19 pax</strong>; cockpit door: <strong>&gt; 45,500 kg or &gt; 60 seats</strong>.</li>\n<li>First-aid kits <strong>1/2/3/4</strong> per 100 pax; megaphones <strong>1 (61–99), 2 (100+)</strong>; crash axe <strong>&gt; 9 seats</strong>, second <strong>&gt; 200</strong>.</li>\n<li>Oxygen: 10% pax beyond <strong>30 min</strong> at 10,000–13,000 ft; <strong>10-min</strong> supply above 25,000 ft.</li>\n<li>FDTL: <strong>8 h / 11 h / 6 landings</strong>; cumulative <strong>35/7, 100/28, 1,000/365</strong>; records <strong>18 months</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":18,"title":"Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","href":"chapters/reg-18.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>MMEL: manufacturer; MEL: operator, approved by DGCA, ≥ as restrictive.</li>\n<li><strong>0.4 mm</strong> of frost spoils lift; cold-soaked wings ice above 0 °C OAT.</li>\n<li><strong>90%</strong> of bird strikes below <strong>500 m</strong>.</li>\n<li>Noise abatement not below <strong>800 ft</strong>; not with crosswind <strong>&gt; 15 kt</strong>, tailwind <strong>&gt; 5 kt</strong>, wet runway; landing configuration by <strong>OM / 5 NM</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fire classes A wood, B liquids, C electrical, D metals; halon never on D; water extinguisher red, CO₂ black, halon green.</li>\n<li>Explosive decompression <strong>&lt; 0.5 s</strong>; masks drop <strong>13,200–14,000 ft</strong>; descend to <strong>10,000 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li>Wind shear criteria <strong>25/500, 40/1,000, 50/1,500</strong>; microburst <strong>≤ 15 min</strong>, peak <strong>2–4 min</strong>.</li>\n<li>Heavy <strong>≥ 136 t</strong>, Light <strong>&lt; 7 t</strong>. Heavy→Light arrival <strong>3 min</strong>, departure <strong>2 min</strong>; distance <strong>6 NM</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fuel dump <strong>≥ 6,000 ft</strong>; others 10 NM, or 1,000 ft above / 3,000 ft below.</li>\n<li>Wet runway LDA <strong>115%</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":19,"title":"Communications","href":"chapters/reg-19.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>QDM = heading <strong>to</strong> the station; QDR = magnetic bearing <strong>from</strong> it; QTE = <strong>true</strong> bearing from it.</li>\n<li>Priority: <strong>distress, urgency, DF, flight safety, met, regularity</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>ROGER</strong> never answers a read-back or yes/no question; <strong>GO AHEAD</strong> deleted; OVER/OUT not used on VHF.</li>\n<li>Flight-number call signs <strong>can't be abbreviated</strong>.</li>\n<li>Readability <strong>1–5</strong> (3 = with difficulty). Test ≤ <strong>10 s</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>121.5/243 MHz</strong>, <strong>2182 kHz</strong>, <strong>500 kHz</strong>; ELT <strong>406 MHz</strong>.</li>\n<li>VHF comms <strong>118–137 MHz</strong>; SSR <strong>1030 up, 1090 down</strong>; LOS <strong>1.25(√h₁+√h₂)</strong>.</li>\n<li>HEAVY on first contact; SUPER for A380. CPDLC log-on <strong>10 min</strong> before the FIR.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":20,"title":"Aircraft Accident and Incident","href":"chapters/reg-20.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Accident window: <strong>boarding → disembarking</strong>. Fatal = within <strong>30 days</strong>. Serious injury = hospital <strong>&gt; 48 h</strong> within <strong>7 days</strong>; burns <strong>&gt; 5%</strong>.</li>\n<li>Damage limited to one engine, a tyre, or from <strong>bird strike/hail</strong> is <strong>not</strong> an accident.</li>\n<li>Crew using <strong>emergency oxygen</strong>, any <strong>fire</strong>, crew <strong>incapacitation</strong> = serious incidents.</li>\n<li>Notify <strong>AAIB + DGCA within 24 h</strong>; DM and police in India. Investigation by <strong>State of Occurrence</strong> (India: <strong>AAIB</strong>).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":21,"title":"Facilitation","href":"chapters/reg-21.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Passenger manifest <strong>not normally</strong> required; stores and baggage lists <strong>never</strong>; max <strong>3 copies</strong>; no visa/fee for aircraft documents.</li>\n<li>Crew visa waived with a <strong>CMC</strong> when on duty.</li>\n<li>India: customs GD + cargo manifest; immigration GD + passenger manifest; health GD. Duty-free stay <strong>6 months</strong>. Export cargo <strong>4 working hours</strong> early.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":22,"title":"Security","href":"chapters/reg-22.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Annex 17 = security; Extraordinary Assembly <strong>1970</strong>; hijacking focus until <strong>1985</strong>.</li>\n<li>Seized aircraft <strong>retained</strong> on landing unless lives are at stake; notify Registry, Operator, affected States and <strong>ICAO</strong>.</li>\n<li>Disruptive passengers board <strong>first</strong>; re-screen after mixing.</li>\n<li>India: <strong>BCAS</strong>; Commissioner of Security (CA); NCASP; Aircraft (Security) Rules <strong>2011</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":23,"title":"Human Performance and Limitations","href":"chapters/reg-23.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Human factors in about <strong>73%</strong> of accidents; <strong>three out of four</strong> accidents from human performance.</li>\n<li>Accident rate <strong>1 per million</strong> movements; most common type <strong>CFIT</strong>.</li>\n<li>Most accidents: <strong>approach and landing</strong>, although it is only about 15% of flight time.</li>\n<li>No oxygen at all: death in <strong>5–8 minutes</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":24,"title":"CRM, TEM and LOFT","href":"chapters/reg-24.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>SHEL: <strong>Edwards 1972</strong>; <strong>Liveware at the centre</strong>; errors arise at the <strong>interfaces</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tenerife <strong>1977</strong> gave birth to CRM programmes.</li>\n<li>Threats are <strong>external</strong>; errors are the crew's. Five error types; wilful = <strong>intentional non-compliance</strong>.</li>\n<li>Steep gradient = dominant captain; shallow = two captains.</li>\n<li>LOFT is <strong>non-jeopardy</strong>: no pass/fail. CRM and LOFT improve the quality of <strong>crew</strong> performance.</li>\n<li>Automation <strong>relocates</strong> the potential for error, generally <strong>increases</strong> workload and creates <strong>new forms of error</strong>. <strong>GPWS</strong> (1980s) cut hull losses.</li>\n<li>Checklist error = <strong>L–S</strong>; cramped cockpit = <strong>L–H</strong>; noise or jet lag = <strong>L–E</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":25,"title":"Aviation Psychology","href":"chapters/reg-25.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>STM <strong>10–20 s</strong>, <strong>7 ± 2</strong>; iconic memory <strong>0.5–1 s</strong>; LTM unlimited with retrieval problems.</li>\n<li>Anderson: <strong>cognitive, associative, autonomous</strong>. Motor programmes live in <strong>LTM</strong>.</li>\n<li>Divided attention = several mental activities at almost the same time. Cocktail party = picking up relevant information unintentionally.</li>\n<li>Hypo-vigilance: monotony, tiredness, sleep need, lack of stimulation; fix with rest periods. Hyper-vigilance = <strong>panic</strong>.</li>\n<li>High arousal <strong>narrows attention</strong>; optimum lower for cognitive tasks.</li>\n<li>Error rate <strong>1/100 → 1/1,000</strong>. Startle recovery <strong>5–10 s</strong>. Acceptable workload ≈ <strong>60%</strong>.</li>\n<li>Design for <strong>90%</strong> (5th–95th). Most important design requirement: <strong>standardisation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Stress-free score <strong>&lt; 60</strong>. Stress boosts <strong>physical strength</strong>. Visible signs include <strong>fast</strong> breathing and dilated pupils.</li>\n<li>Lowest body temperature <strong>04:30</strong>; free-running clock <strong>25 h</strong>; resync <strong>1–1.5 h/day</strong>; <strong>east is harder</strong>.</li>\n<li>Stage 2 ≈ <strong>50%</strong> of sleep; stage 4 = deep sleep; nap inertia up to <strong>20 min</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":26,"title":"Aviation Physiology","href":"chapters/reg-26.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Gas laws: Boyle trapped gas, <strong>Henry DCS</strong>, Dalton hypoxia, <strong>Graham gas exchange</strong>, Charles temperature.</li>\n<li>O₂ % never changes; <strong>partial pressure</strong> does. 18,000 ft ½, 36,000 ft ¼; 8,000 ft 76%.</li>\n<li>Oxygen from <strong>10,000 ft</strong>; 100% at 33,700 ft; pressure breathing above 40,000 ft; boiling above <strong>63,000 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hypoxia types: <strong>CO = anaemic</strong>, <strong>G = stagnant</strong>, <strong>alcohol/cyanide = histotoxic</strong>. Night vision suffers from 5,000 ft.</li>\n<li>Hyperventilation below 10,000 ft: assume it isn't hypoxia; slow breathing 10–12/min.</li>\n<li>Alcohol 24 h; 6,000 ft doubles the effect. Blood donation 48 h; diving 24 h; decompression 12 h; local 12 h, general 48 h; dental 48–72 h.</li>\n<li>TUC FL350 30 s–1 min, halved by rapid decompression.</li>\n<li>Bends joints, creeps skin, chokes lungs, staggers brain.</li>\n<li>Rods 100 M, cones 6 M; rods 30 min, cones 7 min. Myopia concave, hypermetropia/presbyopia convex.</li>\n<li>Otoliths linear, canals angular; steady turn undetected. Ear trouble on <strong>descent</strong>.</li>\n<li>Upslope/narrow/black hole → <strong>low</strong> approach; downslope/wide/bright → <strong>high</strong>.</li>\n<li>60° bank 2 g; 45° 1.41 g. Any epileptic fit bars a licence. Subtle incapacitation more dangerous.</li>\n</ul>"}],"styles":{"concept":565,"numeric":230,"cause":21,"definition":36,"not":48,"code":27,"fill":5},"n":932,"ex":{"concept":{"q":"To fly VFR in class E airspace:","o":["A clearance and/or two-way radio is required","A clearance is required","Two-way radio communication is not required"],"a":2,"ch":5},"numeric":{"q":"A visual approach can be flown:","o":["On VFR flights with visibility 5 km or more","On IFR flights if the cloud base is 1,000 ft above the DA/MDA","On IFR flights, when the pilot has continuous visual reference to the terrain"],"a":2,"ch":7},"cause":{"q":"High arousal overload results in:","o":["A real danger of attention becoming narrowed.","Attention becoming sharpest.","An optimum arousal for more difficult or intellectual (cognitive) tasks."],"a":0,"ch":24},"definition":{"q":"\"Cabotage\" refers to:","o":["Domestic air services","A national carrier","Crop spraying"],"a":0,"ch":2},"not":{"q":"A test call shall not last more than:","o":["8 seconds","12 seconds","10 seconds"],"a":2,"ch":19},"code":{"q":"An abbreviated PAPI (APAPI) is:","o":["Ten units on one side: a wing bar of four with a longitudinal line of six","Twenty units in two wing bars of four with longitudinal lines of six","A wing bar of 2 sharp-transition units, normally on the left of the runway"],"a":2,"ch":12},"fill":{"q":"The otoliths detect ___ and the semicircular canals detect ___.","o":["Angular acceleration, linear acceleration.","Equilibrium, orientation","Linear acceleration, angular acceleration."],"a":2,"ch":26}}},"hb":{"name":"Aviator's Handbook","traps":[{"n":1,"title":"Aerodynamics Basics","href":"chapters/hb-01.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Double the speed → 4× dynamic pressure</strong> (and 4× lift/drag at the same AoA).</li>\n<li><strong>IAS → CAS (position) → EAS (compressibility) → TAS (density)</strong>. All V-speeds CAS except <strong>VA = EAS</strong>.</li>\n<li>Constant IAS climb: TAS <strong>up</strong>; constant TAS climb: Mach <strong>up</strong>. LSS = <strong>39√T</strong> kt, 661 kt at MSL.</li>\n<li>Viscosity of air <strong>rises</strong> with temperature; of liquids <strong>falls</strong>. Kinematic viscosity rises with height.</li>\n<li>Higher RN: higher <strong>CLmax and stalling angle</strong>; turbulent layer resists separation.</li>\n<li><strong>AC fixed at 25%</strong> chord (subsonic); <strong>CP moves forward</strong> with AoA on a cambered wing, fixed on a symmetric one, <strong>backward</strong> on a flat plate.</li>\n<li>Aspect ratio = <strong>b²/S</strong>; wash-out = less incidence at the tip.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":2,"title":"Airflow, Lift and Drag","href":"chapters/hb-02.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Stagnation point: <strong>V = 0, maximum pressure</strong>, total = static.</li>\n<li>Boundary layer = flow <strong>&lt; 99%</strong> of free stream; <strong>2 mm at LE, 20 mm at TE</strong>. Turbulent layer: <strong>more friction but resists separation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Transition moves <strong>forward</strong> with roughness and speed.</li>\n<li>Positive camber lift curve cuts the axis <strong>left of the origin</strong>; symmetric through the origin.</li>\n<li>Sweepback: <strong>higher stall AoA, lower CLmax</strong>. Small LE radius: <strong>abrupt</strong> stall. Roughness aft of <strong>20% chord</strong>: no effect on CL.</li>\n<li><strong>V in L = CL ½ρV²S is TAS.</strong> Same IAS → same lift and drag at any height. 2× speed → CL to <strong>¼</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Profile drag = skin friction + form</strong>. Parasite ∝ <strong>V²</strong>, weight-independent; induced ∝ <strong>1/V²</strong>, ∝ <strong>CL²</strong>, ∝ <strong>1/AR</strong>.</li>\n<li>Strongest wake: <strong>heavy, clean, slow</strong>. Vortices sink ~<strong>1000 ft</strong>, last up to <strong>9 nm</strong>.</li>\n<li>VMD at <strong>Dp = Di</strong>, ≈ 4° AoA, best L/D; <strong>VMP = 0.76 VMD</strong>; <strong>1.32 VMD</strong> = jet range. More weight → VMD <strong>up</strong>; flaps/gear → VMD <strong>down</strong>.</li>\n<li>Ground effect within <strong>½ span</strong>: less induced drag, float, <strong>nose-down</strong>, ASI/altimeter <strong>under-read</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":3,"title":"Stalls, Spins and Upsets","href":"chapters/hb-03.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Stall = <strong>critical AoA</strong>, same at every speed, weight and attitude; buffet comes from wake on the <strong>tailplane</strong>.</li>\n<li>Vs ∝ <strong>√W</strong> (20% heavier → 10% faster); ∝ <strong>√n</strong> (4 g → 2×); bank <strong>60° → ×1.4</strong>, 45° → ×1.2, 30° → ×1.07.</li>\n<li>Flaps, power on, slipstream → <strong>lower</strong> Vs; CG forward, ice, compressibility, forward sweep → <strong>higher</strong> Vs.</li>\n<li>Same <strong>IAS</strong> stall at all heights (density only) — stalling <strong>TAS</strong> rises; compressibility raises it above ~20,000 ft.</li>\n<li>Ice/snow <strong>+30% Vs</strong>, −30% lift, +40% drag; frost <strong>+15%</strong>.</li>\n<li>Deep stall = <strong>swept wing + T-tail</strong> → stick pusher. Shock stall ≈ <strong>M 0.82</strong>. Delta super-stall ≈ <strong>40°</strong> AoA.</li>\n<li>Autorotation: <strong>down-going wing at higher AoA</strong> → less lift, more drag. Don't use aileron at the stall.</li>\n<li>Swept-wing tip stall → <strong>CP forward → pitch-up</strong>; fences, vortilons, saw-tooth, VGs.</li>\n<li>Spin: aerodynamic <strong>roll pro</strong>, pitch and yaw anti; inertial <strong>pitch pro</strong>. Recovery: throttle closed, ailerons neutral, <strong>full opposite rudder, pause, stick forward</strong> — spin may tighten first.</li>\n<li>UPRT: <strong>&gt; 25° up, &gt; 10° down, &gt; 45° bank</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":4,"title":"Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","href":"chapters/hb-04.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Laminar-flow section: max thickness <strong>≈ 50%</strong> chord vs <strong>25–30%</strong> conventional.</li>\n<li>High-speed aerofoil: <strong>low camber, sharp LE</strong>, max thickness ~50%. Symmetric: <strong>no CP movement</strong>.</li>\n<li>Elliptical: least induced drag but <strong>no stall warning</strong>. Rectangular: <strong>root</strong> stalls first. High taper/pointed/swept: <strong>tip</strong> stall.</li>\n<li>Sweep: lift from <strong>V cos Λ</strong>; lower CLmax, higher stall speed, tip stall, pitch-up, Mach tuck; good in turbulence.</li>\n<li>Forward sweep: tip → root vortex, <strong>root stalls first</strong>, needs <strong>aero-elastic tailoring</strong> and canards.</li>\n<li>Slender delta vortex lift: <strong>+30% CL</strong>, CP to <strong>≈ 50%</strong>.</li>\n<li>Canard: wing stalls first → <strong>stability</strong> lost; canard first → <strong>control</strong> lost.</li>\n<li><strong>CDi ∝ 1/AR</strong>; low AR → high stalling AoA, high roll rate.</li>\n<li>Slats: CLmax <strong>+70%</strong>, <strong>raise</strong> stalling AoA, <strong>no camber change</strong>. Krueger: camber, <strong>no slot</strong>. TE flaps: <strong>lower</strong> stalling AoA, <strong>nose-down</strong> pitch, <strong>worse L/D</strong>, AC unchanged.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":5,"title":"Stability","href":"chapters/hb-05.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Longitudinal stability is about the <strong>lateral axis</strong> (tailplane); lateral stability about the <strong>longitudinal axis</strong> (wings); directional about the <strong>vertical axis</strong> (fin).</li>\n<li>CG forward = more stable, heavier controls; CG aft = less stable, lighter. <strong>Static margin = aft CG limit to neutral point.</strong></li>\n<li>Tailplane incidence <strong>less</strong> than wing incidence (longitudinal dihedral). Wing camber: <strong>no effect</strong> on longitudinal stability. Flaps and high power reduce it.</li>\n<li>Negative static stability ⇒ <strong>always</strong> negative dynamic stability.</li>\n<li><strong>Phugoid</strong>: speed and height change at ~constant AoA, easy to control. <strong>Short period</strong>: AoA and g change, hard to control, PIO.</li>\n<li>Ventral fin: + directional, <strong>− lateral</strong>; dorsal fin: + both. Fuselage: directionally <strong>destabilising</strong>.</li>\n<li>High wing, dihedral, sweepback, fin: <strong>+ lateral</strong>; low wing, anhedral, ventral fin, flaps: <strong>−</strong>. <strong>10° sweep ≈ 1° dihedral.</strong></li>\n<li>Spiral = directional <strong>&gt;</strong> lateral; Dutch roll = lateral <strong>&gt;</strong> directional → yaw damper, correct with ailerons.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":6,"title":"Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","href":"chapters/hb-06.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Ailerons = rate control</strong>; elevator and rudder = <strong>displacement</strong> controls.</li>\n<li>Adverse yaw: nose yaws <strong>away</strong> from the roll — from <strong>induced drag of the down-going aileron</strong>. Differential: <strong>up aileron moves more</strong>. Frise: up-aileron's <strong>nose drops into the airflow</strong>.</li>\n<li>Roll due to yaw is in the <strong>same direction</strong> as the yaw.</li>\n<li>Balance tab: <strong>opposite</strong>, lighter, less effective. Anti-balance: <strong>same</strong>, heavier, more effective. Servo tab moved by the pilot; spring tab fixes the servo tab's low-speed weakness.</li>\n<li>Trim tab moves <strong>opposite</strong> to the control surface; re-trim after speed, power or CG change.</li>\n<li>Inset hinge ≤ <strong>20–25%</strong> of chord; CP ahead of hinge = <strong>overbalance</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mass balance prevents flutter</strong>, not stick force.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":7,"title":"Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","href":"chapters/hb-07.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Climb: <strong>lift &lt; weight</strong>, thrust &gt; drag. <strong>Angle ← excess thrust (Vx); rate ← excess power (Vy).</strong> Jet Vx = <strong>VMD</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Wind never changes ROC or ROD</strong> — only gradients and ground distances.</li>\n<li>Glide range: <strong>VMD</strong>, independent of <strong>weight</strong> (heavier = faster, same distance). Glide endurance: <strong>VMP</strong>.</li>\n<li>Turn: <strong>n = 1/cos φ</strong> (60° = 2 g); <strong>r ∝ V²</strong>, <strong>rate ∝ 1/V</strong>; radius independent of weight at same TAS/bank.</li>\n<li>Min radius/max rate: <strong>CLmax, low wing loading, sea level</strong>.</li>\n<li>Range: props <strong>VMD</strong>, jets <strong>1.32 VMD</strong> (1.19 in cruise climb). Endurance: props <strong>VMP</strong>, jets <strong>VMD</strong>. LRC: <strong>+4% speed, 99% range</strong>.</li>\n<li>Step climb: <strong>±2000 ft</strong> of optimum, <strong>4000 ft</strong> steps, <strong>99%</strong> of cruise-climb efficiency.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":8,"title":"High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","href":"chapters/hb-08.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>LSS depends <strong>only on temperature</strong> (39√T). Constant-TAS climb → Mach <strong>up</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mcrit</strong>: first local <strong>M 1.0</strong>; more AoA/weight → <strong>lower</strong> Mcrit. Transonic <strong>Mcrit–1.2</strong>; hypersonic &gt; 5.</li>\n<li>First shock forms on the <strong>upper surface</strong>; shock stall from separation behind it; CP at <strong>≈ 50%</strong> supersonic.</li>\n<li>Normal shock: no direction change, <strong>subsonic</strong> behind, big energy loss. Oblique: direction changes, may stay supersonic. Expansion: speeds up, no loss.</li>\n<li>Supersonic flow <strong>accelerates in a divergent</strong> duct.</li>\n<li><strong>Mach tuck</strong> = CP back → nose-down → Mach trimmer. <strong>Area rule</strong> = waisted fuselage against interference/wave drag.</li>\n<li>Supercritical aerofoil: <strong>flat top, blunt nose, aft camber</strong>; weaker, later shock; lower low-speed CLmax.</li>\n<li>At high Mach: longitudinal stability <strong>up</strong>, lateral and directional <strong>down</strong>.</li>\n<li>Envelope: CLmax line = stall not overstress; <strong>50%</strong> structural margin; heavier → <strong>lower g</strong> allowed.</li>\n<li><strong>Aileron reversal</strong> (wing twist, cure inboard ailerons); <strong>divergence</strong> (CP ahead of flexural axis, no control input); <strong>flutter</strong> (control CG behind hinge → <strong>mass balance</strong>).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":9,"title":"Propellers","href":"chapters/hb-09.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Blade angle: chord ↔ <strong>plane of rotation</strong>; AoA: chord ↔ <strong>relative airflow</strong>; helix angle: plane of rotation ↔ <strong>effective path</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Slip = geometric − effective pitch</strong>. <strong>EMP &gt; GMP.</strong></li>\n<li>Blade angle <strong>decreases towards the tip</strong> (twist).</li>\n<li>Fine for take-off; coarse for cruise; CSU increases blade angle as TAS rises. Overspeed → <strong>coarsen</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>CTM → fine; ATM → coarse</strong>; ATM &gt; CTM normally; windmilling ATM <strong>adds</strong> to CTM.</li>\n<li>Windmilling prop: <strong>more drag</strong> than feathered/stopped.</li>\n<li>Efficiency = <strong>THP ÷ SHP</strong>; &gt; 6 blades → efficiency falls; tips must stay subsonic.</li>\n<li>Clockwise prop: torque → <strong>roll left, left wheel loaded</strong>; slipstream and P-factor → <strong>yaw left</strong>; tail-up → yaw left.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":10,"title":"Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","href":"chapters/hb-10.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Proof = 1.125 × DLL</strong> (no permanent set); <strong>ultimate = 1.5 × DLL</strong> (no collapse).</li>\n<li>Strain has <strong>no units</strong>; stiffness = stress/strain.</li>\n<li><strong>Fail-safe</strong> = redundant load paths + inspections; <strong>safe-life</strong> = fixed life then replace.</li>\n<li>Airliner fatigue counts <strong>pressurisation cycles</strong>. <strong>Miner's rule: Σ n/N = 1</strong>. Crack growth stage begins at ~<strong>1 mm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Spar = main spanwise; ribs = shape; stringers = stiffen skin; frames = hoop stress. <strong>Monocoque: skin takes all loads.</strong></li>\n<li>Accumulator: store, damp, expand, emergency. Reservoir pressurised against <strong>cavitation</strong>. Emergency supply from the <strong>reservoir bottom</strong> (below the stack pipe).</li>\n<li>Constant-pressure pump: <strong>no accumulator needed</strong>. Open system: <strong>one service at a time</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fluid colours: <strong>mineral red</strong>, synthetic <strong>blue</strong>. Milky = air in it.</li>\n<li>Pneumatics: HP bleed at <strong>low RPM</strong>; water trap at the <strong>lowest point</strong>; methanol anti-freeze.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":11,"title":"Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","href":"chapters/hb-11.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Tricycle mains carry <strong>~90%</strong>. Tail-dragger mains <strong>ahead of CG</strong> → ground-loop risk. Quadricycle: crosswind-sensitive.</li>\n<li>Oleo: <strong>gas absorbs, oil damps</strong>. Never fully extended or compressed in use.</li>\n<li>Anti-skid off below <strong>~20 kt</strong>; electrical gives ~<strong>15%</strong> better braking; touchdown and locked-wheel protection.</li>\n<li>Over-inflation wears the <strong>centre</strong>; under-inflation the <strong>shoulders</strong> (main cause of failure). <strong>Red dot = light spot.</strong> Ply rating = <strong>strength</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tubeless: ~<strong>7.5%</strong> lighter, <strong>no creep</strong>. Fusible plugs: <strong>controlled deflation</strong> after hot brakes.</li>\n<li>Shimmy → <strong>broken torque link</strong>; twin nose wheels/Marstrand tyres.</li>\n<li>Aquaplaning ≈ <strong>9√P</strong> kt; higher pressure → higher aquaplaning speed; persists to lower speed once started.</li>\n<li>Power-assisted = <strong>reversible</strong>, natural feel; fully powered = <strong>irreversible</strong>, needs <strong>artificial feel</strong> (Q-feel ∝ ½ρV²). <strong>Jack stall</strong>: actuator can't beat air loads.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":12,"title":"Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","href":"chapters/hb-12.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Cabin temperature <strong>18–24 °C</strong>, humidity <strong>30%</strong>, CO ≤ <strong>1:20,000</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Air cools by expanding through a turbine doing work.</strong> Bootstrap (brake turbine) <strong>can't cool on the ground</strong>; turbo-fan can.</li>\n<li>Isobaric <strong>8000 ft</strong> (airliners); hypobaric <strong>25,000 ft</strong> (fighters). Warning at <strong>10,000 ft</strong> cabin. Max cabin rate <strong>1500 fpm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Pressurisation: <strong>constant inflow, variable outflow</strong>; outflow valve closes → differential up.</li>\n<li>Inward relief at <strong>−0.5 to −1 psi</strong>; valves above the <strong>flotation line</strong>; pre-pressurise <strong>0.1 psi</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-ice prevents; de-ice removes.</strong> Boots = de-ice; hot bleed = anti-ice. Rosemount = vibrating rod.</li>\n<li>Prop de-ice: <strong>inner third</strong>, timer cycling, only with props turning.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":13,"title":"Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","href":"chapters/hb-13.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Matrix transfers load to the fibres. Composites: <strong>moisture, lightning, impact</strong> weaknesses; aero-elastic tailoring cures <strong>divergence</strong>.</li>\n<li>Octane: <strong>iso-octane 100, heptane 0</strong>.</li>\n<li>Colours: <strong>AVGAS 80 red, 100 green, 100LL blue, Jet A-1 clear/straw</strong>.</li>\n<li>Jet A-1: SG <strong>0.8</strong>, flash <strong>38 °C</strong>, wax ≈ <strong>−47/−50 °C</strong>; Jet B wide-cut: flash <strong>−20 °C</strong>.</li>\n<li>Vapour lock worsens with <strong>altitude</strong> (lower boiling point); booster pumps prevent it.</li>\n<li>Vent space <strong>2%</strong>; LP cock at the <strong>firewall</strong>; baffles stop surge.</li>\n<li>Life jackets <strong>50 nm</strong>; rafts <strong>120 min/400 nm</strong>; ELT <strong>2 if &gt; 19 pax</strong>; emergency lights <strong>30 min</strong>.</li>\n<li>Oxygen masks drop at <strong>14,000 ft</strong>; diluter-demand gives <strong>100% at 34,000 ft</strong>; no oil or grease on oxygen fittings.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":14,"title":"Piston Engines","href":"chapters/hb-14.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>CR = (swept + clearance) ÷ clearance.</strong> Petrol ~<strong>10:1</strong>, diesel ~<strong>22:1</strong>.</li>\n<li>Otto = <strong>constant volume</strong>; diesel and gas turbine = <strong>constant pressure</strong>.</li>\n<li>Four-stroke: <strong>720°</strong> per cycle; two-stroke 360°.</li>\n<li>BHP = IHP − friction; THP = BHP × η(prop). Piston engine ≈ <strong>28%</strong> efficient.</li>\n<li><strong>Valve overlap</strong> around TDC, both open → better volumetric efficiency.</li>\n<li>Turbo: exhaust-driven, <strong>waste gate open at sea level</strong>, closes with height. Intercooler reduces detonation.</li>\n<li>Rich <strong>8:1</strong> (cool, take-off), lean <strong>20:1</strong> (hot, cruise, detonation risk); correct <strong>15:1</strong>.</li>\n<li>Carb ice: up to <strong>22 °C</strong> drop; <strong>+30 to −10 °C</strong>; fixed pitch → RPM drop; CSU → <strong>MAP</strong> drop.</li>\n<li>Magneto is <strong>independent of the battery</strong>; mag check: small drop, engine keeps running.</li>\n<li>Detonation = explosion after the spark (lean, hot, low octane); <strong>pre-ignition = before the spark</strong> (hot spots).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":15,"title":"Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","href":"chapters/hb-15.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Gas turbine = <strong>Brayton, constant pressure</strong>; actual ≈ <strong>50%</strong> of ideal efficiency.</li>\n<li><strong>Highest pressure: combustion inlet; highest temperature: combustion outlet; hottest part: NGVs.</strong> EGT at <strong>LP turbine outlet</strong>. Bleed on → EGT ↑, EPR ↓.</li>\n<li>Thrust = <strong>ṁ(Vj − Va) + A(Pj − P₀)</strong>; maximum at the <strong>start of the take-off roll</strong>; recovers above ~500 kt (ram).</li>\n<li>Propulsive efficiency = <strong>2Va ÷ (Vj + Va)</strong> → large mass, low velocity is efficient.</li>\n<li>Bypass ratio = <strong>bypass ÷ core</strong>; low &lt; 1.5, high &gt; 1.5. Turbofan: quieter by 20–25%, better SFC.</li>\n<li>Turboprop best below <strong>350 kt</strong>; max thrust <strong>stationary</strong>. Free turbine: <strong>easy start</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Alpha: lever → fuel, CSU → RPM. Beta: lever → blade angle, governor → fuel.</strong></li>\n<li>Synchronise = same RPM; <strong>synchrophase</strong> = same RPM <strong>plus</strong> set blade phase.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":16,"title":"Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","href":"chapters/hb-16.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Subsonic intake = <strong>divergent</strong> duct; <strong>super-critical</strong> = starving (take-off), <strong>sub-critical</strong> = spilling. Ram recovery = intake pressure back to ambient.</li>\n<li>Supersonic intake: oblique shocks then <strong>one normal shock</strong>; delivers ~<strong>M 0.5</strong>; <strong>intake buzz</strong> = shock swallowed/expelled.</li>\n<li>Side intakes need <strong>BL diverters</strong>.</li>\n<li>Compressor stage = rotor + stator; ~50/50 pressure rise; stator: V ↓, P ↑, T ↑.</li>\n<li>Centrifugal <strong>≈ 4:1/stage</strong>, FOD-tolerant; axial <strong>1.2:1/stage</strong>, annulus narrows rearwards; ratio = <strong>product</strong> of stages.</li>\n<li><strong>Stall: low axial velocity → high blade AoA.</strong> Surge = all stages; signs <strong>EGT ↑, RPM ↓, bangs</strong>. Cures: VIGVs, VSVs, bleed valves, multi-spool, acceleration control.</li>\n<li>Air split <strong>20/20/60</strong>; flame speed <strong>2–4 m/s</strong>; annular chamber shortest.</li>\n<li>Duplex burner: two orifices; vaporisers need starting sprayers.</li>\n<li>NGVs: convergent, <strong>pressure → velocity</strong>, choked, hottest. <strong>Impulse root, reaction tip.</strong> TET ≈ <strong>1700 °C</strong>.</li>\n<li>Creep: primary, secondary (steady), tertiary (fracture). Film cooling beats convection.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":17,"title":"Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","href":"chapters/hb-17.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Convergent nozzle <strong>chokes at M 1</strong>; con-di nozzle gives <strong>supersonic</strong> exhaust; variable nozzle <strong>opens</strong> with reheat to prevent surge.</li>\n<li>Turbofan noise from the <strong>fan</strong>; turbojet from jet velocity.</li>\n<li>Cold-stream reverser = <strong>fan air</strong>. Reversers: no inadvertent deployment, no re-ingestion.</li>\n<li>Reheat at <strong>100% RPM</strong>; SFC ↑ (efficient only near M 2.5); turbofan reheat ~50%; water–methanol up to ~30%.</li>\n<li>APU: battery-started, <strong>not paralleled</strong>. High-energy ignition <strong>12 J</strong> (start/relight); low-energy <strong>6 J</strong> (continuous).</li>\n<li><strong>Hot start</strong> = EGT over limit (tailwind, residual fuel); <strong>hung</strong> = no self-sustaining RPM (starter/battery); <strong>wet</strong> = no light-up (igniter) → dry motor.</li>\n<li><strong>OSG</strong> = overspeed; <strong>BPCU</strong> = less fuel with altitude; <strong>MBPU</strong> = minimum burner pressure, idle RPM rises with height.</li>\n<li>Oil pressure <strong>after the pump</strong>; temperature after the <strong>cooler</strong>; scavenge pumps return oil; <strong>chip detectors</strong> warn of bearing wear. Aviation = <strong>dry sump</strong>.</li>\n<li>FADEC: <strong>two channels, no manual override</strong>.</li>\n<li>Ramjet: no compressor/turbine, <strong>needs speed</strong>, efficient ~M 2, limit ~M 5; scramjet = supersonic combustion, hydrogen. Pulse-jet: thrust at zero speed, noisy.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":18,"title":"Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","href":"chapters/hb-18.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Inductor: current lags voltage 90° (CIVIL: in a Capacitor I leads V, V leads I in an inductor L).</strong> Inductor passes DC; capacitor blocks DC.</li>\n<li>X_L <strong>rises</strong> with frequency, X_C <strong>falls</strong>; equal at resonance. Capacitors add in <strong>parallel</strong>, resistors in <strong>series</strong>.</li>\n<li>Right-hand rule = <strong>generator</strong>; left-hand = <strong>motor</strong>.</li>\n<li>Induced EMF grows with <strong>speed, flux, turns</strong>. Lenz → back EMF.</li>\n<li>Soft iron = temporary; hard steel = permanent. Aircraft magnetism from <strong>hammering/vibration in the earth's field</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Series cells: volts add; parallel: Ah add.</strong> Ni-Cd 1.2 V, KOH, <strong>thermal runaway</strong>; lead-acid 2 V, H₂SO₄. Capacity check <strong>3-monthly, ≥ 80%</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":19,"title":"AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","href":"chapters/hb-19.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>RMS = 0.707 × peak.</strong> <strong>f = (poles/2) × RPM/60</strong> → 8 poles × 6000 RPM = <strong>400 Hz</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>PF = true ÷ apparent power</strong>; resistive PF = 1.</li>\n<li><strong>X_L = 2πfL ↑ with f; X_C = 1/(2πfC) ↓ with f.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Alternator: rotating field, stationary armature</strong>; regulate by <strong>field current</strong>; aircraft <strong>115/200 V, 3-phase, 400 Hz</strong>. Star: V_line = <strong>1.73 V_phase</strong>.</li>\n<li>Synchronous motor: speed set by <strong>frequency</strong>, not self-starting, used in <strong>tachometers</strong>. Induction motor: <strong>slip</strong> ~5%.</li>\n<li><strong>Commutator</strong> turns AC to DC in a DC generator; <strong>interpoles</strong> fix armature reaction; <strong>reverse-current relay</strong> stops the battery motoring the generator.</li>\n<li><strong>Series motor: high torque, overspeeds unloaded</strong> (starters, actuators); <strong>shunt motor: constant speed</strong>.</li>\n<li>Transformer: AC only; turns ratio = voltage ratio. <strong>Inverter DC → AC; TRU AC → DC.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Voltmeter parallel/high R; ammeter series/low R.</strong> Single-pole = airframe return.</li>\n<li>AC paralleling needs <strong>frequency, phase, voltage</strong> matched; split bus doesn't.</li>\n<li><strong>Trip-free CB cannot be held in on a fault.</strong> Fuse and CB both <strong>in series</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bonding = equal potential</strong>; static wicks on <strong>trailing edges</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":20,"title":"Pitot-Static Instruments","href":"chapters/hb-20.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Pitot = total pressure; static vent <strong>perpendicular</strong>; ASI/Mach use both; altimeter/VSI <strong>static only</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Static blocked: altimeter freezes, VSI zero, ASI under-reads in a climb.</strong> <strong>Pitot blocked (drain too): ASI over-reads in a climb</strong> (acts as an altimeter).</li>\n<li>Alternate static in a pressurised cabin → altimeter shows <strong>cabin altitude</strong>.</li>\n<li>Altimeter lag: <strong>under-reads in a climb</strong>. Colder than ISA or high → low pressure: <strong>over-reads</strong> (dangerous). Cold correction ≈ <strong>4% per 10 °C</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>DA = PA ± 118 × ΔISA</strong>; TA ≈ <strong>4 ft/°C/1000 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li>VSI = direct static vs <strong>metered (leaking) static</strong>; IVSI removes <strong>lag</strong>.</li>\n<li>ASI compressibility: <strong>over-reads</strong> at high speed. <strong>White arc VS0–VFE, green VS1–VNO, yellow VNO–VNE, red VNE, blue VYSE.</strong></li>\n<li>Machmeter has <strong>no temperature/density error</strong>; works on <strong>(P − S)/S</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":21,"title":"Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","href":"chapters/hb-21.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Compass needs <strong>H</strong>, must ignore <strong>Z (dip)</strong>; deadbeat. Acceleration error <strong>zero on N/S</strong>; turning error <strong>zero through E/W</strong>; both zero at the magnetic equator.</li>\n<li><strong>ANDS</strong> (accelerate → north in NH) and <strong>UNOS</strong> (turning through north under-reads/lags).</li>\n<li><strong>B = (E − W)/2, max E/W, corrected on east; C = (N − S)/2, max N/S, corrected on north; A = mean of eight, lubber line.</strong></li>\n<li>Flux valve: <strong>400 Hz in, 800 Hz out</strong>; <strong>3 legs</strong>; Hooke's joint, free in pitch/roll, fixed in azimuth; in the <strong>wing tip/tail</strong>.</li>\n<li>Rigidity ↑ with <strong>RPM and moment of inertia</strong>; precession rate ∝ torque ÷ (I × ω), 90° on in the direction of spin.</li>\n<li>Drift = horizontal plane; topple = vertical plane. Apparent drift <strong>15 sin lat °/h</strong>; transport <strong>(E/W GS × tan lat)/60</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>DHAT2, THAR1, AVE2.</strong> TSI: rate 1 = <strong>3°/s</strong>; low RPM → under-reads.</li>\n<li>Ball <strong>out = skid</strong>, ball <strong>in = slip</strong>.</li>\n<li>Air-driven AH on acceleration: <strong>false climb + left bank</strong>. Erection cut-out above <strong>10° bank</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":22,"title":"Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","href":"chapters/hb-22.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>RA: <strong>FMCW, 4300 MHz</strong>, two aerials, 0–2500 ft, <strong>frequency difference ∝ height</strong>; no use for separation or terrain ahead.</li>\n<li>ADC inputs <strong>pitot, static, TAT</strong>; reduces lag, corrects position and compressibility errors.</li>\n<li>Altitude alert: <strong>900 ft</strong> box, <strong>±300 ft</strong> deviation alert; inhibited on glideslope/gear down.</li>\n<li>Stall warning <strong>5 kt / 5%</strong>; inputs AoA, flaps/slats, gear, speed.</li>\n<li>Thermocouple = <strong>Seebeck</strong>, self-powered (CHT, EGT); resistance bulb = Wheatstone bridge (oil temp).</li>\n<li>Bourdon tube = <strong>high</strong> pressure; capsule = low.</li>\n<li>Mechanical (drag-cup) tacho needs <strong>no power</strong>; tacho probe needs power.</li>\n<li>Capacitance gauge: fuel is the dielectric; compensated → <strong>mass</strong>.</li>\n<li>Oil torque meter = <strong>helical gear + oil pressure</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":23,"title":"INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","href":"chapters/hb-23.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>IRU = <strong>3 gyros + 3 accelerometers</strong>; integrate <strong>twice</strong> (acceleration → velocity → distance); needs <strong>TAS</strong> for wind.</li>\n<li>Align <strong>stationary</strong>; gyro-compassing finds <strong>latitude and true north</strong> but <strong>not longitude</strong>; quicker at the equator. NAV before moving; off/on in flight → <strong>ATT only</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Schuler period 84.4 min</strong>, max error at <strong>42.2 min</strong>. Initial azimuth misalignment is <strong>unbounded</strong>; accelerometer bias <strong>bounded</strong>. <strong>Kalman filter</strong> blends sources.</li>\n<li>RLG = <strong>Sagnac</strong>, counter-rotating beams, frequency difference; <strong>lock-in</strong> cured by <strong>dither</strong>; &lt; 10 min alignment.</li>\n<li>Yaw damper prevents <strong>Dutch roll</strong>; auto-trim <strong>pitch only</strong>. <strong>Fail-passive</strong>: no autoland after failure; <strong>fail-operational</strong>: lands anyway.</li>\n<li>EFIS colours: <strong>magenta active route</strong>, cyan inactive, amber caution, red warning, green engaged.</li>\n<li>Nav database <strong>28 days</strong>, no frequencies; <strong>DME/DME</strong> best radio fix; <strong>cost index 0 = max range/min fuel</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":24,"title":"Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","href":"chapters/hb-24.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>c = fλ</strong>; 121.5 MHz ≈ 2.47 m. Double range → <strong>4× power</strong>.</li>\n<li>Polarisation = plane of the <strong>electric</strong> field. Higher frequency → <strong>less</strong> refraction, diffraction; <strong>more</strong> attenuation.</li>\n<li>Surface wave up to ~50 MHz, better at <strong>low frequency</strong>. VHF range ≈ <strong>1.23 (√h₁ + √h₂)</strong> nm; refraction +7%; ducting up to +40% (inversion, high pressure, moist surface).</li>\n<li>Sky wave <strong>2–30 MHz</strong>; at night layers rise → <strong>lower frequency</strong>. Higher frequency → longer skip and dead space.</li>\n<li>AM: longer range, narrower band; FM: clearer. <strong>VHF voice A3E (DSB); HF J3E (SSB)</strong>; SSB needs a <strong>BFO</strong>.</li>\n<li>HF working frequency ≈ <strong>0.85 MUF</strong>. SELCAL <strong>4 letters A–S, no I/N/O</strong>.</li>\n<li>VHF comm <strong>117.975–137 MHz, 8.33 kHz</strong>. INMARSAT 4 satellites, <strong>80°N–80°S</strong>.</li>\n<li>Superhet: mix with <strong>local oscillator</strong> → <strong>IF</strong>. Yagi: reflector longer, directors shorter.</li>\n<li>VDF classes <strong>A ±2°, B ±5°, C ±10°</strong>; QDM vs QTE.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":25,"title":"NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","href":"chapters/hb-25.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>NDB: <strong>vertically polarised</strong>, LF/MF; A1A needs a <strong>BFO</strong>, A2A ident is a <strong>1020 Hz tone</strong>. Range ≈ <strong>2√P land, 3√P sea</strong>. DOC <strong>day only</strong>.</li>\n<li>ADF: loop = <strong>figure-of-eight</strong>, sense aerial = <strong>circle</strong>, together a <strong>cardioid</strong> (one null).</li>\n<li>Night effect up to <strong>30°</strong>, worst dawn/dusk; <strong>quadrantal error at 45° relative bearings</strong>; coastal refraction least at <strong>90°</strong>; thunderstorm needle points at the <strong>CB</strong>; <strong>no failure warning</strong>.</li>\n<li>VOR: <strong>30 Hz reference FM on 9960 Hz</strong> + <strong>30 Hz variable AM</strong>; in phase at <strong>magnetic north</strong>; phase difference = radial. <strong>Horizontally polarised.</strong> D-VOR swaps AM/FM and cuts <strong>site error</strong>.</li>\n<li>VOT: needle reads <strong>180° (radial 360 from)</strong>; tolerance ±4° ground, ±6° airborne. VOR system ±5°.</li>\n<li>CDI ±10° full scale, <strong>2°/dot</strong>. VOR range = <strong>1.23 (√h₁ + √h₂)</strong>.</li>\n<li>DME: <strong>UHF 962–1213 MHz, reply ±63 MHz, 50 µs delay</strong>, <strong>slant range</strong>, ~<strong>100 aircraft</strong>, ±0.25 nm/1.25%.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":26,"title":"ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","href":"chapters/hb-26.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Localiser <strong>90 Hz left/150 Hz right</strong>, glidepath <strong>90 Hz above/150 Hz below</strong>. LOC odd-tenth 108.1–111.95 MHz; GP 329–335 MHz (UHF).</li>\n<li>Coverage: LOC <strong>±10° 25 nm, ±35° 17 nm</strong>; GP <strong>±8°, 10 nm, 0.45θ–1.75θ</strong>. Full scale ±700 ft at threshold. TCH ≈ <strong>50 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li>Markers <strong>75 MHz</strong>: <strong>outer blue dashes 400 Hz</strong>, <strong>middle amber dot-dash 1300 Hz</strong>, <strong>inner white dots 3000 Hz</strong>.</li>\n<li>False glideslope ~<strong>6°</strong> → intercept <strong>from below</strong>. 3° path: <strong>300 ft/nm</strong>, ROD ≈ <strong>5 × GS</strong>.</li>\n<li>MLS <strong>5 GHz, 200 channels, ±40°, curved approaches</strong>.</li>\n<li>Primary radar: <strong>max range ↑ as PRF ↓</strong>; min range ↓ with shorter pulse; range resolution = pulse width; bearing resolution = beam width; 2× range = <strong>16× power</strong>.</li>\n<li>Doppler groundspeed and drift; <strong>sea bias under-reads</strong>; Janus cancels pitch error.</li>\n<li>Weather radar <strong>9375 MHz (3 cm)</strong>; red = heavy; <strong>magenta = turbulence</strong>; steepest gradient = worst; cosecant beam for mapping; can't see CAT.</li>\n<li>SSR <strong>1030 up / 1090 down</strong>; <strong>4096 codes</strong>; mode C <strong>100 ft</strong>, mode S <strong>25 ft</strong>; garbling <strong>1.7 nm</strong>; <strong>7500/7600/7700</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":27,"title":"GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","href":"chapters/hb-27.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>GPS: <strong>24 satellites, 6 planes, 55°, 20,180 km, 12 h</strong>; master control <strong>Colorado Springs</strong>; <strong>L1 1575.42 MHz</strong>; <strong>4 satellites</strong> for 3-D fix + clock. Datum <strong>WGS-84</strong>.</li>\n<li>RAIM <strong>5</strong> (or 4 + baro); FDE <strong>6</strong> (or 5 + baro). GBAS = airport, VHF, precision; SBAS = geostationary (GAGAN in India).</li>\n<li>Biggest GPS error: <strong>ionospheric</strong>. SA off since <strong>2000</strong>.</li>\n<li>GPWS modes: <strong>1 sink rate, 2 terrain closure, 3 loss after take-off, 4 too low gear/flaps, 5 below glideslope, 6 call-outs/bank, 7 windshear</strong>. EGPWS adds <strong>look-ahead (TAD, TCF)</strong>.</li>\n<li>GPWS action: wings level, <strong>full power</strong>, climb at <strong>Vx</strong>.</li>\n<li>TCAS II: <strong>vertical RAs only</strong>; follow RA over ATC; TA = <strong>no manoeuvre</strong>; RA response <strong>5 s</strong>. Symbols: hollow cyan (other), solid cyan (proximate 6 nm/1200 ft), <strong>amber circle TA</strong>, <strong>red square RA</strong>.</li>\n<li>ADS-B broadcast (1090 ES / 978 UAT); ADS-C contract (periodic, demand, event). CPDLC: keep voice watch.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":28,"title":"Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","href":"chapters/hb-28.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>DOM = BEM + crew, catering, water…</strong> (no usable fuel, no TL). <strong>ZFM = DOM + TL</strong>; <strong>TOM = ZFM + fuel</strong>; LM = TOM − trip fuel.</li>\n<li>MTOM = lowest of <strong>MZFM + fuel, RTOM, RLM + trip fuel</strong>. Max TL = lowest of <strong>MZFM − DOM, RTOM − DOM − fuel, RLM − DOM − remaining fuel</strong>.</li>\n<li>Crew standard masses <strong>85/75 kg</strong>. Weigh every <strong>4 years</strong>; fleet <strong>9 years</strong>; reweigh if DOM changes <strong>0.5% MLM</strong> or CG <strong>0.5% MAC</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>CG = Σ moments ÷ Σ mass</strong>; <strong>%MAC</strong> formula. Forward CG: more stable, heavier controls, higher Vs, <strong>less range</strong>; aft CG: opposite.</li>\n<li><strong>TODA = TORA + clearway; ASDA = TORA + stopway.</strong> Clearway ≤ <strong>½ TORA</strong>.</li>\n<li>Screen height <strong>35 ft</strong> (A take-off), <strong>50 ft</strong> landing. Wind factors <strong>50% head / 150% tail</strong>.</li>\n<li>TORR/TODR factor <strong>1.15</strong> (all engines). LDR jets <strong>× 1.67</strong>, props <strong>× 1.43</strong>, wet <strong>+15%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>10% mass → 21% take-off distance; 10% landing speed → 20% LDR.</strong></li>\n</ul>"},{"n":29,"title":"V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","href":"chapters/hb-29.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>VMCG ≤ V1 ≤ VR</strong>; V1 ≤ VMBE. <strong>VR ≥ 1.05 VMCA, 1.1 Vs</strong>. <strong>V2 ≥ 1.2 Vs and 1.1 VMCA</strong>. VREF = <strong>1.3 Vs</strong>.</li>\n<li>VMCA: <strong>≤ 5° bank towards the live engine</strong>, aft CG. VMCG: rudder only, 30 ft deviation.</li>\n<li><strong>Vx = Vy at the absolute ceiling.</strong> Service ceiling <strong>100 fpm piston / 500 fpm jet</strong>.</li>\n<li>Gross = fleet average (certification); <strong>net = gross − margin</strong> (obstacles).</li>\n<li>Class A = <strong>all multi-jets</strong> + turboprops &gt; 5700 kg/9 pax.</li>\n<li>More flap: <strong>field limit up, climb limit down</strong>. Hot/high: both down. <strong>2nd segment 2.4%</strong> (twin) is critical. Net take-off path −0.8% (twin), clear by <strong>35 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Balanced field: ASDR = TODR</strong>; higher V1 → ASDR up, TODR down.</li>\n<li>En-route net −1.1% (twin); obstacles <strong>1000/2000 ft</strong>. Drift-down at best L/D; wind changes distance not rate.</li>\n<li>Approach climb <strong>2.1%</strong> (OEI, twin), landing climb <strong>3.2%</strong> (AEO).</li>\n<li>Coffin corner: stall TAS meets Mcrit; 1.3 g buffet margin.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":30,"title":"Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","href":"chapters/hb-30.html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Brain <strong>2% weight, 20% energy</strong>; <strong>70%</strong> information visual; <strong>73%</strong> accidents human factors.</li>\n<li><strong>Henry</strong> (dissolved gas) → DCS; <strong>Dalton</strong> (partial pressures) → hypoxia. Breathing driven by <strong>CO₂</strong>. CO: <strong>250×</strong>, headache.</li>\n<li>Memory: iconic <strong>1 s</strong>, echoic <strong>2–8 s</strong>, STM <strong>7 ± 2</strong>, 10–20 s.</li>\n<li>Ideal leader <strong>P+ G+</strong>; autocratic <strong>P− G+</strong>.</li>\n<li>Sleep: cycle <strong>90 min</strong>, credit <strong>+2/−1</strong>, max <strong>16</strong>; circadian <strong>25 h</strong>; REM = dreams.</li>\n<li><strong>Cones: colour, day, fovea; rods: night, periphery, 30 min adaptation.</strong> Hypoxia hits night vision first. Myopia → concave lens.</li>\n<li><strong>Semicircular canals: angular; otoliths: linear.</strong> Threshold ~2°/s. Barotrauma worst in <strong>descent</strong>.</li>\n<li>Noise damage &gt; <strong>90 dB</strong>; pain <strong>140 dB</strong>.</li>\n<li>+Gz: grey-out → black-out; <strong>−Gz: red-out, no protection</strong>. +Gz tolerance ~4.5–6 g, 8 g with suit.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":31,"title":"Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","href":"chapters/hb-31.html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Eyes give ~90% of orientation.</strong> <strong>Type 1 (unrecognised)</strong> disorientation is the worst. Remedy: trust instruments, 60 s straight and level.</li>\n<li><strong>Coriolis</strong> = head movement in a turn; <strong>leans</strong> = sub-threshold roll; <strong>somatogravic</strong> = acceleration feels like <strong>pitch-up</strong> (night go-around danger).</li>\n<li>Up-slope or narrow runway → <strong>feel high → fly low</strong>; down-slope or wide → feel low → fly high. <strong>Black hole → below the glidepath.</strong></li>\n<li>Autokinesis = fixed light seems to move; empty-field myopia focuses 1–2 m.</li>\n<li>DCS: <strong>nitrogen</strong>, above ~<strong>25,000–30,000 ft</strong>; bends/creeps/chokes; no flying <strong>12 h</strong> after diving (<strong>24 h</strong> if &gt; 30 ft).</li>\n<li>Hypoxia types: <strong>hypoxic</strong> (altitude), <strong>anaemic</strong> (CO), <strong>stagnant</strong> (g, cold), <strong>histotoxic</strong> (alcohol). Stages: indifferent <strong>10,000</strong>, compensatory <strong>15,000</strong>, disturbance <strong>20,000</strong>, critical.</li>\n<li>TUC: <strong>20,000 ft ~10 min, 25,000 ft ~3 min, 30,000 ft ~1 min</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hyperventilation = <strong>CO₂ washed out</strong>, tingling; recover by slow breathing; <strong>if in doubt at altitude, it's hypoxia</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"styles":{"definition":8,"concept":642,"numeric":224,"not":17,"cause":44,"code":11},"n":946,"ex":{"definition":{"q":"Density is defined as:","o":["Weight per unit area","Force per unit area","Mass per unit length","Mass per unit volume"],"a":3,"ch":1},"concept":{"q":"A higher Reynolds number gives:","o":["Lower CLmax","Higher CLmax and stalling angle","Earlier separation","A thicker laminar layer"],"a":1,"ch":1},"numeric":{"q":"Cabin humidity is kept at about:","o":["90%","5%","60%","30%"],"a":3,"ch":12},"not":{"q":"A thrust reverser must not allow:","o":["Any reverse thrust","Deployment on the ground","Use on landing","Re-ingestion of debris or hot gas"],"a":3,"ch":17},"cause":{"q":"Roll due to yaw is greatest at:","o":["High altitude only","Low angle of attack","High speed","Low speed and high angle of attack"],"a":3,"ch":6},"code":{"q":"Ply rating on a tyre indicates its:","o":["Number of layers","Pressure","Strength","Speed limit"],"a":2,"ch":11}}}};